WEBVTT

02:56.870 --> 02:59.014
Battery's dead. All right.

03:00.350 --> 03:03.918
We're gonna go ahead and get started. We weren't stalling at all because

03:04.418 --> 03:06.647
I know traffic is bad and some people had to drive a little ways,

03:06.680 --> 03:10.022
but I'm sure we'll hear about that. So we'll go

03:10.055 --> 03:13.424
ahead and call this work session to order. That's the work session of the Eagle

03:13.924 --> 03:17.677
Mountain City Council, May 19th. It is officially 4:03,

03:18.515 --> 03:21.586
and we'll get started with our first discussion item. If you need a

03:22.086 --> 03:24.021
few minutes, I can go to the second one, but we had you first,

03:24.070 --> 03:28.246
so. Okay, I'm gonna go come back to you. So we're

03:28.343 --> 03:32.136
actually gonna go to item 1B, if that's okay, 'cause I know Benjamin's

03:32.636 --> 03:35.737
ready to go. So if that's okay, we'll start. It's a discussion on becoming a

03:35.867 --> 03:38.987
believer city. This is something that we've all been a part of

03:39.084 --> 03:42.300
over time, and I, I think I've talked to you guys about this. I don't

03:42.317 --> 03:45.532
really remember, but Ben's gonna present what it is, and we can give

03:45.565 --> 03:48.785
staff direction or whatever it is that we wanna do. So.

03:53.846 --> 03:56.448
Awesome. How are you today? It's good to see you.

03:58.950 --> 04:02.360
I just wanted to take you back really quick to

04:02.860 --> 04:05.494
about 7 years ago, just to set the stage.

04:07.665 --> 04:10.897
So I had this note in my phone that I wrote down,

04:12.456 --> 04:15.757
and I just wanted to read it to you really quick. It says,

04:15.936 --> 04:19.276
I have a crazy idea. I'm going to run holding signs

04:20.144 --> 04:23.603
to help lift other people as they drive by. I also want to

04:24.103 --> 04:27.961
get others involved and grow it huge. The inspiration came over time,

04:28.749 --> 04:32.269
but today it hit harder with specifics. I wanted to

04:32.769 --> 04:35.629
write it down just in case I forgot. This was back on July 18th,

04:39.336 --> 04:42.583
2019. So eventually I started. I don't know

04:43.083 --> 04:45.164
if all of you know, Some of you do what I do.

04:46.185 --> 04:49.089
I go around the city running with my sign. Everybody's nodding. Okay, good.

04:51.713 --> 04:55.554
It's been such an amazing experience. I have had— I can't

04:56.054 --> 04:58.672
even count anymore. I can't count the stories of the impact it's had on people

04:59.733 --> 05:03.365
very early on, how it's changed people's

05:03.381 --> 05:06.257
lives. But I knew I needed to share it.

05:07.582 --> 05:10.798
And ever since I started doing it, I actually started in 2022. Wow.

05:11.366 --> 05:12.989
Took me a while to get up the courage to do it,

05:14.274 --> 05:17.070
but I knew I had to share it with people, and I wanted to get

05:17.570 --> 05:20.990
other people involved. So what I've done this year— I've been

05:21.006 --> 05:24.235
working for quite a while, but I've launched a nonprofit. It's called Show Me

05:24.735 --> 05:27.802
a Sign, and the point of it is to invest belief at scale,

05:28.718 --> 05:32.493
and to do that through every person and

05:32.993 --> 05:36.638
every organization. And so you can join me as a believer. You can sign

05:36.671 --> 05:39.545
up, and basically you There's a list of actions,

05:40.075 --> 05:43.447
specific things that you do, and you can go and log those. Running is one

05:43.947 --> 05:46.948
of them. You get points, kind of gamifies it. It's a fun thing. And I

05:47.448 --> 05:51.026
have chapters, start chapters at schools and in the community. But really,

05:51.058 --> 05:54.318
I also wanted to involve large organizations like the city.

05:55.458 --> 05:59.359
And so at the city level, what's involved in that are

05:59.568 --> 06:04.032
2 things. One is helping me bring

06:04.064 --> 06:07.227
belief visible in the environment, number one,

06:07.275 --> 06:10.390
and number two is in the people. So two would be the

06:10.890 --> 06:15.384
chapters in the city, working with them to bring events

06:15.884 --> 06:17.937
like, we have this event coming up next month, we did it last year,

06:17.953 --> 06:21.871
the Motivation Mile. We're already doing some of those things as a city. But really

06:22.224 --> 06:25.403
the key one I wanted to talk about today was that first one, was bringing

06:25.903 --> 06:29.658
it into the environment, and how do you do that? And so what

06:30.158 --> 06:33.415
I call that is my Road Signs of Belief initiative.

06:33.463 --> 06:36.842
So at the city level, My nonprofit raises the money

06:37.748 --> 06:42.116
for signs of belief, and then working with the city government

06:42.616 --> 06:46.530
and city officials, getting permission, whether we

06:46.562 --> 06:49.774
have to change policy, whether we have, you know, have to talk about those things,

06:49.871 --> 06:53.164
but getting permission to install those around the city in key locations,

06:53.212 --> 06:56.910
not all over the place, not spamming and making it

06:57.410 --> 07:00.980
become something like wallpaper that you just ignore, but something really impactful.

07:01.190 --> 07:04.474
Yeah. in key areas and having those signs just like the ones

07:04.974 --> 07:08.228
that I hold when I run, but having them available 24/7.

07:08.966 --> 07:12.656
People that are coming home from work late at night, a terrible job

07:13.156 --> 07:16.121
situation, and they see that sign and it changes their day,

07:16.137 --> 07:19.491
it changes their week. It inspires them to do something completely

07:19.991 --> 07:22.731
different than they would have, to not road rage the people in front of them,

07:22.860 --> 07:26.277
to not go on Eagle Mountain City Citizens and post rude

07:26.309 --> 07:29.454
comments. Just think of the impact we

07:29.954 --> 07:33.319
could have with some of these things. Everybody smiled when I said that. You don't

07:33.819 --> 07:36.825
read that group, do you? No, it's a great group.

07:37.548 --> 07:41.019
I love living here. I've lived here for 20 years this year. And so that

07:41.519 --> 07:45.341
is my proposal to Eagle Mountain becoming

07:45.841 --> 07:49.039
the first believer city and helping me do those 2 things,

07:49.299 --> 07:52.381
bring belief into the environment and into the people.

07:55.756 --> 07:57.829
So I don't know how this works. I don't usually come to this. We have

07:58.329 --> 08:02.116
like a Q&amp;A. Yeah, that's, that's perfect. I actually just

08:02.616 --> 08:05.436
sent the website to Lacey to see if you put it on the screen.

08:05.613 --> 08:09.109
Oh, okay, perfect. Yeah, because I just— to show that if

08:09.238 --> 08:12.927
it's possible. If not, it's okay. One of the things that

08:13.427 --> 08:17.499
you'd mentioned earlier was the schools have signed up as a school or

08:17.563 --> 08:20.755
as a club. What I have pulled up is Become

08:20.787 --> 08:24.203
a Believer City, and then it just says sign up now. It says

08:24.236 --> 08:28.776
what is a Believer City, and then some of the activities

08:28.904 --> 08:33.140
and mentions and stuff on the website. So the

08:33.204 --> 08:37.151
question really is, does Eagle Mountain want to become a

08:37.651 --> 08:40.761
Believer City on the website? And there's no cost.

08:41.179 --> 08:44.757
So those are kind of the questions. Can I weigh in here, Mayor? I had

08:45.257 --> 08:49.185
the opportunity to spend, gosh, I don't know, probably too long at Ben's house on

08:49.685 --> 08:52.876
a Saturday or Friday afternoon. I invited you and

08:53.376 --> 08:56.678
he's like, yes, I'll be there. And so we've discussed this in

08:56.742 --> 09:00.175
some detail, and I think that— I

09:00.675 --> 09:03.800
think number one, I want to say that I'm supportive of exploring this idea.

09:03.928 --> 09:07.200
I think that— I think it would be a shame for another city

09:07.216 --> 09:10.986
to become the first city to adopt or to embrace the

09:11.227 --> 09:14.932
I Believe in You mantra that Ben has kind of created.

09:15.878 --> 09:19.503
And so for me at least, I think it's something that we absolutely

09:20.003 --> 09:23.727
should have a real discussion about as a council and and

09:24.227 --> 09:26.298
decide, number one, is it something we should do? And I believe that it is.

09:26.346 --> 09:30.202
So I'm going to say that I'm supportive of that. I think there may

09:30.702 --> 09:34.230
be some challenges with it in some areas as to how do we

09:34.730 --> 09:38.417
do that and how do we remain— I

09:38.676 --> 09:41.845
just, in my mind's eye, there are some questions that I have that we need

09:42.345 --> 09:46.629
to deal with, with legal and other things, right? How are we fair and equal

09:47.063 --> 09:50.994
and even-handed with— other organizations who might have a

09:51.494 --> 09:54.774
similar need or a similar desire to partner with the city. But I think it's

09:55.274 --> 09:58.540
something that we should explore. And, uh, I've had the chance to look at

09:59.040 --> 10:01.204
his website, kind of go through it. I just invite each of you to do

10:01.220 --> 10:03.836
the same and become familiar with it. And I'd really like to see us take

10:03.884 --> 10:07.212
this up in one of our very next off-site meetings and see if we

10:07.712 --> 10:11.175
can't do something with that, and then find some time to support

10:11.675 --> 10:14.005
what he's got going on on this. Is it the 6th of June, right?

10:14.167 --> 10:15.687
Isn't that the date? Yep. Yeah. Yeah,

10:15.848 --> 10:19.671
1:00 PM.

10:19.832 --> 10:22.653
Okay. What's the website? It's showmeasign.org.

10:23.040 --> 10:23.443
Yeah.

10:27.715 --> 10:31.531
I guess my question is how we

10:32.031 --> 10:35.735
apply what we do, I feel like can come later

10:35.881 --> 10:39.154
in the mix. Where and if signs are going in a certain

10:39.186 --> 10:42.604
place, all of those other things. But opting

10:42.636 --> 10:45.899
to be a believer city, it feels

10:45.931 --> 10:48.375
like a no-brainer to me, so I don't want to delay it at all.

10:48.889 --> 10:52.295
I think it'd be best if we— if that's what we want to do,

10:52.376 --> 10:55.257
that we move forward boldly and just take care of that, and then we can

10:55.290 --> 10:58.673
figure out the rest and how we apply it

10:58.916 --> 10:59.386
as a body.

11:05.207 --> 11:07.006
Yeah, so if you click on Become a Believer,

11:08.717 --> 11:12.093
Button there, and then we're a city or town.

11:15.110 --> 11:18.038
So this page just goes through, it gives a kind of an overview,

11:18.119 --> 11:20.934
kind of a pitch, right? This is what it means,

11:22.244 --> 11:24.979
and this is why we need to do it, and then these are the benefits.

11:26.102 --> 11:29.386
And then there's actually a guide at the bottom you can go through. There's an

11:29.483 --> 11:31.150
audio overview at the top. So everything's here.

11:33.605 --> 11:36.694
to walk you through. And I understand there's challenges,

11:36.743 --> 11:39.121
right? I mean, you can't just have everybody going around putting signs, and you have

11:39.621 --> 11:42.887
to make sure it's done in a way that's legal, that's safe. Again,

11:42.903 --> 11:46.465
I wanted to reiterate, it's not something the city would pay for. I would raise

11:46.513 --> 11:49.615
the money myself through my nonprofit. I would just need to

11:50.115 --> 11:51.515
work with the administration side of it.

11:53.620 --> 11:56.483
Can you scroll back up just where it shows that sign on the side of

11:56.516 --> 11:58.813
the road? Oh, at the top?

11:59.686 --> 12:03.068
Keep going. Right there. Is that an example

12:03.568 --> 12:07.331
of the kind of signs you're looking to put? Yeah. So, okay. Yeah. So I

12:07.831 --> 12:11.215
have something I put a lot of thought into. It's called the Language of

12:11.232 --> 12:15.203
Belief. So there's another page on my site that

12:15.703 --> 12:19.004
you can go to and see. Basically,

12:19.426 --> 12:22.255
I feel really passionate about this. The best thing that I could say to you,

12:22.287 --> 12:25.775
if I had one, if I had 2 seconds with you, right? The most

12:26.275 --> 12:28.649
powerful thing I could say to you is I believe in you. I believe that's

12:29.149 --> 12:32.663
the best thing I could say. If I tell you I loved you, you didn't

12:32.679 --> 12:36.854
know me very well, right? It would be kind of weird. So I believe

12:37.354 --> 12:40.836
that's the best type of language to use is, hey, I believe in your potential

12:40.949 --> 12:44.754
to do something better. And so I believe in phrases that

12:46.151 --> 12:48.977
are of that ilk, right? Of a belief base. And so I actually have a

12:49.477 --> 12:52.761
whole list of phrases that I've rated. I have a scoring system and everything

12:52.794 --> 12:55.039
that you can go look at on the page as well, Language of Belief.

12:56.090 --> 12:59.789
But I would suggest that you look at those phrases

12:59.902 --> 13:03.179
and choose the ones that are highly scored there because I've seen them have the

13:03.679 --> 13:06.714
most impact. That was my next question. Yeah. I put a lot of thought into

13:07.214 --> 13:09.397
it. There's a lot of signs you could put up. So, we can just take

13:09.897 --> 13:13.034
a look at what the messages are. Yeah. Even the placement of where they are.

13:13.259 --> 13:18.128
I think that would be great. I like this idea. I think it's There

13:18.628 --> 13:21.607
is power in words, and sometimes, you know, nobody else can get through to a

13:21.671 --> 13:25.487
person, and something as simple as seeing something like that sparks something in

13:25.599 --> 13:29.318
you that allows you to take that

13:29.430 --> 13:32.252
next step. I think that— I think it's a great idea.

13:32.877 --> 13:36.308
I'd love to go through the number of

13:36.324 --> 13:39.834
places that we're talking about putting them. I think key locations

13:39.850 --> 13:43.185
would be great. I think this embodies What I

13:43.217 --> 13:46.807
think Eagle Mountain is, what I've been trying to push forward

13:46.824 --> 13:50.623
for the Eagle Mountain way, ways that we can show that we

13:50.847 --> 13:53.941
care about our community, that we're here for each other, that we're neighbors

13:54.438 --> 13:57.483
working together. I think this is a great way. I love that,

13:57.499 --> 14:00.721
thank you. I keep trying to touch the screen, but it's a screen share.

14:02.212 --> 14:05.017
I think just what you got on your shirt right there, I believe in you,

14:05.498 --> 14:08.864
that's so recognizable. I don't know why you're

14:09.364 --> 14:13.032
running Cory B. Ride. I was like, that guy's nuts. I'm very

14:13.144 --> 14:16.413
visible and very safe. But I honked at you a couple times, and it is

14:16.590 --> 14:19.830
cool. Like, you're on your way to work, you've had a crazy morning, and you

14:20.330 --> 14:23.741
just see that. It's like, some guy's running out here. It's not comfortable

14:23.871 --> 14:27.687
to run with a sign. What a crazy person. But I think

14:28.187 --> 14:31.872
it's a really cool idea. And around our city,

14:31.953 --> 14:35.624
there are things like the— I'm just thinking of the Eagle Monument— that people—

14:36.219 --> 14:39.532
I really didn't know that people cared that much. Until it was gone.

14:39.644 --> 14:43.300
Until it was gone, I was like, okay, that's like something

14:43.316 --> 14:46.715
that for people is very important. And I think the message that you've carried,

14:46.763 --> 14:50.339
that's pretty like a recognizable brand. In this case,

14:50.451 --> 14:53.995
the city would just be partnering with you on some

14:54.495 --> 14:58.373
parcels of land to put the signs in, is my understanding. Not maintaining

14:58.389 --> 15:02.109
the signs, just put them up. And I

15:02.609 --> 15:05.781
think we've got a lot of young kids that I think that message really resonates.

15:06.734 --> 15:09.827
Thank you. And there's other things you would have to consider, right? Where we put

15:10.327 --> 15:13.773
them. Someone might like to get out and take a picture, right? So don't put

15:13.789 --> 15:17.251
them in precarious places. And just, we'd have to be really strategic

15:17.911 --> 15:20.181
about how we do that. Make sure it's safe, make sure it's clearly visible.

15:20.229 --> 15:22.784
But then again, like you said, let's not put them everywhere.

15:23.723 --> 15:26.408
Let's put 'em in key places that would make a big impact.

15:27.670 --> 15:28.852
And yeah, thank you for that.

15:31.818 --> 15:35.093
So I'm meeting with Cedar Fort later this week to discuss

15:35.593 --> 15:38.915
like a race within the valley here. Cedar Fort

15:38.947 --> 15:42.255
has some need to raise funds for their fire department.

15:42.319 --> 15:45.242
We have some need to raise funds for our volunteer police.

15:46.639 --> 15:49.738
But I was thinking it may be interesting to add an element of this,

15:50.108 --> 15:53.560
of what you have, into that. But I may coordinate with

15:54.060 --> 15:57.639
you offline about that. Very cool. Yeah, I've wanted to do a race. I actually

15:58.139 --> 16:01.569
looked into it a couple years back. Somebody I'm very busy. I don't

16:02.069 --> 16:06.089
make money doing this, right? So I don't want to, but somebody went to organize

16:06.589 --> 16:09.039
a race for me and then they kind of— something happened and they backed out,

16:09.055 --> 16:11.283
so I wasn't able to do it. I think we could put together something that

16:11.783 --> 16:14.729
would be pretty powerful in the, in the valley here. So I'll be in touch

16:15.229 --> 16:18.352
with you on that. Very cool. I'd love that. Thank you. So,

16:18.560 --> 16:22.070
Mayor, if I can just— maybe I can wrap it up. What I'm hearing is

16:22.183 --> 16:25.452
support from the council to move forward

16:25.952 --> 16:29.632
in trying to become Believer City. and maybe working out the details of what that

16:29.762 --> 16:32.258
means as we go forward. Is that a fair assessment?

16:34.180 --> 16:37.635
Yes. Melissa? Yes. Okay. All right. That's exactly what I was wondering

16:37.667 --> 16:40.239
is what do we want to do from here? Is there more discussion or can

16:40.739 --> 16:44.886
we sign up and then work out sign locations? So I think the direction

16:45.386 --> 16:48.798
is to have staff, whoever that is, to go ahead and log on and become

16:48.846 --> 16:51.869
a Believer City. And then as needs come in,

16:52.791 --> 16:56.180
is there any action item required on that? Marcus, are we

16:56.680 --> 17:00.360
okay just to— We can put it on the legislative priority

17:00.860 --> 17:04.760
list. Be generous. You know, I mean, I think if,

17:05.260 --> 17:08.940
uh, the mayor feels comfortable moving forward,

17:09.440 --> 17:13.100
given the, the discussion went, then I think you have the authority to move

17:13.600 --> 17:16.840
forward. I don't think there needs to be a vote on it and be that

17:17.340 --> 17:20.120
formal about it. If that's— yeah, that's why I was making sure. So if that's

17:20.620 --> 17:24.444
the general consensus, I think we'll have Uh, Ben, direct somebody

17:24.765 --> 17:29.130
to look into this. And then as the sign locations, things like that become

17:29.885 --> 17:32.741
needs, we'll have to approve those as they come in, each, each one other,

17:32.757 --> 17:35.646
and just sign it up for now. Mayor, I also think it would be useful

17:35.742 --> 17:39.000
if we were to do like a resolution or a proclamation

17:39.048 --> 17:42.483
at some point down the road. It doesn't have to be tied to signing up,

17:42.547 --> 17:45.998
but kind of declaring that we are a Believer City, I think,

17:46.094 --> 17:48.565
is that way all of the council can vote on that too.

17:49.100 --> 17:52.200
Yeah, I think that would be fun. I was actually thinking earlier today about reading,

17:52.390 --> 17:55.690
you know, like we've done before, today's whatever day,

17:56.190 --> 17:59.410
you know, that today we're recognizing El Monte as a believer

17:59.910 --> 18:02.130
city. So I think that's something we can do in the near future as we,

18:02.630 --> 18:06.110
as we progress. So that works. Thank you

18:06.610 --> 18:10.110
very much. Awesome. Looks like we'll be signing up. Thank you so much.

18:10.610 --> 18:14.220
Thanks for your time. Thanks, Ben. Okay, we're gonna go back to item 1A,

18:14.490 --> 18:18.299
a discussion about tax increment financing and bonding options. And we have with us—

18:19.606 --> 18:22.769
do you want to lead into that? Or I think we can turn it right

18:23.269 --> 18:26.610
over to Laura Lewis, our bond counsel, and she can help us

18:26.727 --> 18:29.903
understand the needs of our bonding.

18:32.210 --> 18:35.441
Thank you for allowing me just a few minutes when I arrived. I appreciate that.

18:36.406 --> 18:40.490
I am Laura Lewis, your municipal advisor with LRB

18:40.990 --> 18:44.533
Public Finance Advisors. Randy Larson with Gilmore

18:44.647 --> 18:49.130
Bell is bond counsel, and they would be preparing all the legal documents

18:49.630 --> 18:53.017
And since we're going to discuss different options of how you might want to secure

18:53.097 --> 18:56.607
the financing, I've asked him to be here if you have any

18:56.623 --> 18:59.846
questions in that regard. Because of our timing, we may

19:00.346 --> 19:04.025
have to move sort of with like a, you know, dual-pronged approach.

19:04.074 --> 19:06.801
So I'm going to hand out— did this— I don't know if I sent this

19:07.301 --> 19:09.876
in time for it to get up on the screen, so I'm so sorry.

19:11.800 --> 19:11.998
Okay.

19:33.388 --> 19:37.145
Did everybody get one? I've got more. I won't be able to sell them afterwards,

19:37.161 --> 19:37.467
so.

19:55.758 --> 20:00.302
All right. Well, we're here to talk about the financing for

20:00.578 --> 20:05.244
a project that's needed in conjunction with a development

20:06.848 --> 20:11.404
within the RDA area. The funds will be used to accelerate

20:11.581 --> 20:15.511
offsite infrastructure improvements of Old Airport Road

20:16.011 --> 20:19.859
and Lake Mountain Corridor, which, you know, if it

20:20.359 --> 20:23.196
weren't for this, they would get built to like the minimum, if you will.

20:24.174 --> 20:27.944
And someone else in the room that's an engineer or whatever

20:28.444 --> 20:31.410
could give you more detail. But otherwise, they would be built to a more minimum

20:31.910 --> 20:34.951
standard. With this financing, they'll be able to build, uh, you know,

20:34.999 --> 20:37.947
the full width, put in turn lanes, and all of those things,

20:37.980 --> 20:40.271
which will be, um, very beneficial for the city.

20:41.264 --> 20:44.869
The, uh, terms of the development agreement with the developer

20:45.369 --> 20:48.698
within the CRA haven't changed. We're not here to discuss any of that. The arrangement's

20:49.198 --> 20:53.088
still the same. The participation will be for the city as well as

20:53.184 --> 20:57.494
the county— different, um, percentages of participation—

20:58.071 --> 21:01.596
but the city and county will each participate by allowing the,

21:01.852 --> 21:05.621
um, a portion of the sales tax generated just off this new development

21:06.166 --> 21:09.117
to support the, the cash flows for the bonds.

21:09.646 --> 21:13.271
Same duration, so everything there that you've talked about before, um,

21:13.383 --> 21:16.687
remains the same. The general finance

21:17.187 --> 21:20.856
plan— so I, um, you know, remind all of my clients

21:21.356 --> 21:25.042
frequently that what we, um, you know, what you identify as the

21:25.090 --> 21:28.516
cash source to repay the debt doesn't necessarily necessarily have to be

21:28.969 --> 21:32.192
what you use as security. They can be one and the same,

21:33.216 --> 21:35.809
probably, well, I'm going to say 80% of the time they are, maybe only 70%,

21:36.292 --> 21:39.802
but sometimes we pledge a certain thing.

21:39.851 --> 21:43.456
So an example might be in Ogden. So if they did a

21:43.777 --> 21:47.683
parking structure, but at the time they weren't collecting parking

21:47.715 --> 21:51.540
revenues, so we pledged the asset as opposed to the

21:52.040 --> 21:55.695
revenue stream. So it doesn't always have to be, you know, what you're actually

21:56.195 --> 21:59.964
financing. So the finance plan here is there's an independent

22:00.464 --> 22:04.267
third-party entity by the name of EPS Denver that is conducting

22:05.006 --> 22:08.282
a study relative to the sales taxes that will be generated

22:08.378 --> 22:11.830
off of— they project will be generated

22:11.846 --> 22:15.379
off of just this project area. And they

22:15.879 --> 22:19.264
have national databases for how much a, you know, fill in the blank, a home

22:19.313 --> 22:22.909
improvement store or grocery store or super mega Walmart

22:23.409 --> 22:27.841
or whatever might produce. That's a lot of money. And they are pulling

22:27.905 --> 22:31.296
that together along with data that they are able to get

22:31.665 --> 22:35.618
locally. Utah law prescribes

22:36.118 --> 22:40.728
that that data be maintained under very strict ways

22:40.744 --> 22:43.540
that it can be delivered to them, that they can use it in studies.

22:44.134 --> 22:48.006
But they are trying to dial down so that you and the bondholders have

22:48.506 --> 22:50.481
a very good idea of what those revenues are likely to be.

22:52.100 --> 22:55.470
We anticipate a delivery of like the numbers

22:55.518 --> 22:58.711
related to that by the end of this month, so in a little over a

22:58.727 --> 23:02.477
week, and the full written report by the middle of

23:02.493 --> 23:06.189
June. The timing is such so that they would

23:06.205 --> 23:10.270
like to get under construction by the end of July, like shovels

23:10.286 --> 23:13.917
in the ground. And so we've got a little bit of a mismatch in terms

23:14.417 --> 23:18.191
of like waiting for all of that final data before you

23:18.691 --> 23:21.875
decide how you'd like to um, finance it. And so we're here

23:22.375 --> 23:24.647
tonight to discuss it so you can sort of see the, you know, the pros

23:24.679 --> 23:27.948
and cons of different options. Um, I did see, uh,

23:27.964 --> 23:31.841
an email that, uh, your city manager sent to you, and I wanted to hire

23:31.954 --> 23:34.053
him because he did a— Well,

23:34.213 --> 23:36.857
that's good for you. Good for you.

23:37.097 --> 23:40.670
He did a flawless job of giving you, uh, information in advance,

23:40.718 --> 23:43.587
so it will allow me to go through this a little bit faster.

23:44.212 --> 23:47.753
So the debt will be structured based on what we see those projected revenues to

23:48.253 --> 23:50.767
be. Our goal will be to structure the debt. If we think the projected revenues

23:50.816 --> 23:53.397
are here, we'll put the debt right in, we'll try to tuck it right in

23:53.926 --> 23:57.694
so that the finance plan will be for those revenues to repay the debt.

23:58.576 --> 24:01.782
So again, with the finance plan, the finance, we know what the revenue is going

24:02.282 --> 24:05.871
to be. Now we have to discuss how you want to backstop it, if you

24:06.371 --> 24:09.510
want to backstop it, to further securitize that loan.

24:10.119 --> 24:14.112
So option 1 will be just to pledge the sales tax

24:14.128 --> 24:17.577
tax increment that is coming off of that project area.

24:18.380 --> 24:22.169
And as Ben described in his information to you,

24:22.699 --> 24:26.376
should we get those, you know, that data back and we go, oh, well,

24:26.986 --> 24:31.273
it looks like for every dollar we have in debt, we have $1.01

24:31.610 --> 24:35.528
in likely revenue, that might be a very difficult bond

24:35.560 --> 24:39.237
to sell, right? These are projections. They are trying to be conservative. We have

24:39.737 --> 24:42.272
been conservative. So Ideally,

24:43.026 --> 24:46.926
in order to sell it as a tax increment bond only,

24:47.038 --> 24:51.002
that's the only thing that we're pledging, then I would like to have

24:51.596 --> 24:55.432
1.5 times coverage. So for every $1.50 you

24:55.464 --> 24:58.706
get in TIF revenue, we would structure it so that your debt

24:58.754 --> 25:03.087
payment would only be $1. Therefore, if there's

25:03.328 --> 25:07.420
a bad disease, we're spreading,

25:07.484 --> 25:10.839
people don't shop as much for a while or whatever, you've got some headroom.

25:10.903 --> 25:14.145
Thank you. Under your development agreement,

25:14.161 --> 25:17.981
you would still capture all of that revenue and

25:18.157 --> 25:21.785
you would seek to set it aside to use it as soon as you

25:22.285 --> 25:25.556
could, depending upon the bond structure, to call the bonds. So it wouldn't

25:26.056 --> 25:29.408
be lost. It just gives some headroom and comfort to, you know,

25:29.569 --> 25:33.116
the bondholders about that revenue stream. Because of that,

25:33.164 --> 25:36.181
you know, as we go through these 3 options, think about if I were buying

25:36.230 --> 25:39.408
that bond, right? If you're the bond purchaser, that's going to

25:39.456 --> 25:42.751
be your highest-risk bond. What do they want for risk?

25:43.252 --> 25:45.190
Brett knows. They want a higher return, right?

25:47.371 --> 25:50.735
So that's the highest-risk bond. You know, we'll structure it so that

25:51.235 --> 25:54.681
it's likely to be very successful, but there's nothing

25:54.795 --> 25:58.963
else behind it. Sales taxes don't come off of that specific project.

25:59.721 --> 26:03.079
Oh dear, right? We're in trouble, right? So that's going to carry

26:03.176 --> 26:07.108
a higher interest rate. I've got a summary table with data on on

26:07.413 --> 26:10.959
their About the Rates here in a little bit. That transaction

26:11.087 --> 26:14.183
also, because of its— it won't carry a rating,

26:14.953 --> 26:18.322
there's a likelihood that we may want to do that as a direct

26:18.822 --> 26:22.189
