26:22.237 --> 26:25.702
and bad with the direct purchase. I just mention it because it's different and the

26:26.184 --> 26:30.098
numbers on here may show a little bit different. That's option 1.

26:30.195 --> 26:33.565
Option 2 is the same Tax

26:34.065 --> 26:36.988
increment pledge. So again, we're still planning on using those revenues,

26:37.663 --> 26:41.487
but instead of just leaving it, you know, alone, the city

26:41.599 --> 26:45.102
can choose to backstop it with your moral obligation.

26:45.858 --> 26:49.087
What that means is the city would adopt a resolution that

26:49.587 --> 26:53.619
says if there are insufficient revenues to make that debt payment,

26:54.053 --> 26:58.166
then we would consider— it does not obligate you— we would consider

26:58.696 --> 27:01.154
making up the differential.

27:02.874 --> 27:06.778
The obligation would require you to put that

27:06.826 --> 27:10.383
shortfall amount into your budget so that you have to actively take

27:10.883 --> 27:14.296
it out, but it doesn't force you to make the payment.

27:14.409 --> 27:17.324
It just is a show of good faith that, you know, they're not just going

27:17.824 --> 27:20.028
to, oh yeah, we said that, but, you know, no one is ever going to

27:20.528 --> 27:22.266
get it on the budget, right? It's a show of good faith that you would

27:22.766 --> 27:26.169
put it in the budget. You could again, very easily and actively take it

27:26.669 --> 27:29.348
out. You're not obligated to do it under that option.

27:30.087 --> 27:33.941
The city's credit is high enough that the bond market would view that very strongly

27:34.037 --> 27:37.553
because they're gonna say, eh, they're not gonna wanna stiff

27:38.053 --> 27:41.632
us on this deal, right? So it's going to sell well, but again,

27:41.841 --> 27:45.245
know that you're not obligated. The third

27:45.341 --> 27:47.460
