38:58.447 --> 39:02.281
but just from an education— I don't— I'm not giving any

39:02.481 --> 39:05.361
investment advice to the council. So I just want to be clear on that.

39:05.426 --> 39:09.804
But I— Laura, thank you for your presentation. That was really succinct and

39:10.060 --> 39:13.765
super helpful. The one question I had was going back to, to the

39:14.265 --> 39:16.924
debt capacity. You mentioned the $82 million.

39:18.175 --> 39:21.799
If, if we were to take out debt, let's say for a city hall or

39:22.299 --> 39:26.434
something not commercially related, yes, is that

39:26.934 --> 39:31.710
something we need to factor into this $82 million number, or could

39:32.210 --> 39:34.988
Would that be outside as like a general obligation? Is this just looking at commercial

39:35.020 --> 39:39.596
tax? This is just looking at sales tax, yes. So if

39:40.096 --> 39:43.787
you were to finance a city hall, a rec center, parks, fire station, all of

39:43.835 --> 39:47.062
those kind of vertical projects,

39:48.090 --> 39:52.088
all of those could be done with a GO vote, could be done

39:52.425 --> 39:56.198
with sales tax as the security. Or it can be

39:56.698 --> 39:59.490
done with the actual physical facility as a security as well.

40:00.309 --> 40:03.504
Now, in order of interest rate,

40:03.520 --> 40:06.186
again, I started with the lowest interest rate is going to be GO bond.

40:06.651 --> 40:10.601
Second will be the sales tax, and those are probably 10

40:11.101 --> 40:13.732
or 15 basis points from each other, so not even a whole quarter percent,

40:13.780 --> 40:18.116
but there will be some difference. And then probably add a quarter percent

40:18.616 --> 40:20.829
or 25 basis points to that if you did it as a building authority.

40:21.967 --> 40:26.390
Building authority is how we secure if

40:26.890 --> 40:30.925
we pledge the real property as the asset. So I'd want to like know,

40:31.358 --> 40:34.082
I mean, have an idea. Nobody knows the future, right? But I'd want to know

40:34.582 --> 40:38.617
like, okay, is the plan to finance a city hall and

40:38.649 --> 40:41.566
then a rec center? If we had some idea of timing, right? Rec center,

40:43.040 --> 40:46.645
the rating agencies will knock you down because they don't

40:47.145 --> 40:49.962
view view it as essential. Now I have looked at them, you know me well

40:50.462 --> 40:54.610
enough, I have looked at them and said citizens would rather a rec

40:55.110 --> 40:58.423
center than a cool city hall as a general

40:58.923 --> 41:02.446
rule. But they don't care. They view a city hall as essential. Fire station

41:02.462 --> 41:05.715
is essential. Police station is essential. So if we were, you know,

41:05.731 --> 41:08.823
if we wanted to say, okay, we don't want all of our sales tax capacity

41:08.839 --> 41:12.541
used up, we want, you know, the physical facility to act

41:13.041 --> 41:16.291
as security to one or more of these. We could do it as a building

41:16.307 --> 41:20.089
authority, keep your sales tax capacity freed up, if you

41:20.297 --> 41:23.422
would. And we also— this nice thing we don't do in the,

41:23.502 --> 41:26.691
you know, personal or commercial lending

41:26.707 --> 41:30.649
world, but local governments, we can cross-collateralize your assets.

41:31.146 --> 41:34.079
So let's say you wanted to do a park and it's like, oh, well,

41:34.159 --> 41:37.748
you know, bond market thinks that's nonessential. You'd still get a good rating, but you

41:37.780 --> 41:41.197
wouldn't get as of rating if it were a city

41:41.229 --> 41:45.705
hall. Well, we can— or a fire station. We can finance

41:46.205 --> 41:49.893
a fire station and the park. They're cross-collateralized. You make the lease

41:50.393 --> 41:53.759
payment on one or you make the lease payment on either of them. Similar with

41:54.259 --> 41:57.690
city hall. So you have various options. So in a worst-case scenario,

41:57.754 --> 42:01.572
we could max out the $82 million

42:02.150 --> 42:05.333
for commercial-related activities and in in addition to that, do a GO

42:05.446 --> 42:08.931
bond for a city hall or— okay. Yes. I just wanted

42:09.431 --> 42:13.352
to be clear on that without impacting our credit rating. Yes. The second question

42:13.852 --> 42:16.364
I had, I was just looking at, and it may just be a difference in

42:16.864 --> 42:20.793
the credit agencies. It looks like you're using S&amp;P. I was looking at Fitch

42:21.018 --> 42:24.324
this morning and it had, I guess these are 15-year bonds,

42:24.405 --> 42:28.431
but the 5-year AA I think was like at 3.7%.

42:28.512 --> 42:32.835
We're at 4.1%. How would you compare

42:32.867 --> 42:36.497
those? I always hedge when I'm showing numbers in the public.

42:36.577 --> 42:39.388
So our rate could be better than what you're showing here.

42:39.645 --> 42:43.564
Okay. To that point, I had a conversation with my colleague

42:44.064 --> 42:46.792
that does all my quantitative analysis, and I'm like, ooh,

42:46.921 --> 42:51.129
because he had that, the one with the sales tax backs off about a 384

42:51.820 --> 42:54.422
or something in that neighborhood. And I'm like, ooh, Nate,

42:54.871 --> 42:58.194
have you read the news? today, right? I mean, because it's just

42:58.694 --> 43:01.934
a matter— I mean, if we had been marketed today, right? Yes, I think we

43:02.434 --> 43:05.962
would be under 4. Okay. But because we're not marketing today,

43:06.042 --> 43:08.979
I put a little bit of wiggle room in it. Again,

43:09.171 --> 43:12.879
these aren't guaranteed rates or anything, but just again for the purpose

43:13.007 --> 43:16.682
of, you know, not getting too dialed down and having

43:16.779 --> 43:19.956
markets change and people go, well, you had on this piece of paper.

43:20.743 --> 43:25.364
So I, I have been some Okay, and then maybe

43:25.460 --> 43:28.492
just 2 more quick questions. We have the ability to prepay.

43:28.749 --> 43:32.487
Is there a limited amount of time that we could pay? It depends

43:32.744 --> 43:36.771
on the structure. So if we did a direct purchase, the difference between direct

43:37.271 --> 43:40.493
purchase and a public offering would be if we did a direct

43:40.541 --> 43:44.471
purchase, we would put together a term sheet and submit

43:44.971 --> 43:47.568
it on your behalf. It would state right at the top that we are acting

43:47.584 --> 43:50.807
as your municipal advisor, and purchasers would know that.

43:51.368 --> 43:54.734
We would submit it to purchasers, I think most of which,

43:54.798 --> 43:58.181
maybe one, that we have a list of probably 12 or

43:58.681 --> 44:01.900
so that we're sending those out to. I think all but one

44:01.948 --> 44:04.962
of them would be a bank, bank that you'd be familiar with their name.

44:06.196 --> 44:09.691
If that direct purchaser were to buy the bonds, we don't

44:09.707 --> 44:13.330
need an underwriter. We don't need an offering document. We still need a

44:13.830 --> 44:17.080
plethora of legal documents, but we don't need an offering document.

44:18.937 --> 44:22.867
Typically, with direct purchase,

44:24.273 --> 44:27.814
they will— some of the purchasers will view it as having an edge

44:28.089 --> 44:30.200
if they allow the bonds to be callable at any time.

44:30.925 --> 44:33.936
So it's one of the things that in addition to rate, they'll say, okay,

44:33.952 --> 44:37.367
we'll give you rate X and we'll allow it to be callable at

44:37.867 --> 44:41.590
any time. Another entity may say, we'll give you rate, you know,

44:41.751 --> 44:45.392
X minus, but we won't allow it to be

44:45.601 --> 44:48.088
callable. So it's one of the things, if we do a direct purchase, we get

44:48.588 --> 44:52.131
all the stuff back. We, you know, put together a nifty little spreadsheet

44:52.631 --> 44:56.109
so that you can see all the, you know, options, if you will, and we

44:56.189 --> 44:59.575
work with the city, your finance director and city manager, to choose what's going to

45:00.075 --> 45:03.264
be best for you. In that instance, I think

45:03.764 --> 45:07.273
it would be highly likely that they would be callable Maybe not any time,

45:07.321 --> 45:10.206
but I bet we could at least get down to a 5-year call, maybe a

45:10.706 --> 45:12.446
1-year call without a penalty.

45:14.616 --> 45:16.166
If we do a public offering,

45:18.174 --> 45:21.628
the standard currently is to have 10-year par bonds,

45:21.742 --> 45:25.131
so we wouldn't be able to prepay them until year 10. For a decade.

45:26.440 --> 45:30.109
We can— we've been able to negotiate with— when bonds are being structured

45:30.609 --> 45:33.322
with 5% coupons, we can usually get away with a 9-year par call.

45:34.322 --> 45:37.227
If you said, I want to have them callable sooner, well,

45:37.388 --> 45:41.165
every year I come down, that's going to drive your interest rate up. That changes

45:41.439 --> 45:45.320
in the market, and typically, it doesn't

45:45.385 --> 45:48.911
get very cost-effective to get too demanding beyond like a 7-year.

45:49.909 --> 45:53.439
You really start to lose the edge you might

45:53.811 --> 45:57.147
think you are going to get by having that bond callable, because you're paying more

45:57.196 --> 46:01.231
in yield for it. Okay. Thank you. Can I ask one

46:01.263 --> 46:05.133
clarifying question, Mayor? Laura, how is MET calculated?

46:05.213 --> 46:08.344
Is it part of the sales tax revenue calculation, or is it

46:08.844 --> 46:12.358
a separate consideration? Can you see the deer

46:12.858 --> 46:16.035
in the headlights? Clearly it's separate.

46:16.758 --> 46:20.065
Okay, and that's what I thought. I just wanted to make sure that I was

46:20.129 --> 46:23.485
thinking correctly, that it is separate, because that's also another revenue stream.

46:23.726 --> 46:27.111
That could be pledged towards something, perhaps if the city decides to

46:27.611 --> 46:31.218
go in a different direction. Oh, municipal energy tax. Hello.

46:31.748 --> 46:35.117
I just— I didn't— I was— as you could see,

46:35.133 --> 46:38.005
I was like, MET. I was thinking tax terms.

46:38.053 --> 46:41.358
Municipal energy tax. Yes, you can. You can also pledge

46:41.454 --> 46:44.631
that. We can pledge that in conjunction with or

46:44.855 --> 46:48.561
separate too. So let's say you just use, you know, the sales tax

46:48.770 --> 46:52.540
here. Then you could pledge your municipal energy tax

46:53.040 --> 46:56.160
to a park to support a park bond. So it was a good question.

46:56.320 --> 46:59.160
I'm sorry I wasn't up to speed on my acronyms. So the municipal energy tax

46:59.660 --> 47:03.080
is calculated into this— into the graphs that you provided?

47:03.580 --> 47:07.180
It is not included. That is correct. Thank you.

47:07.680 --> 47:12.020
Laura. Yes. You're going to throw another acronym at me? No, I hate them.

47:12.520 --> 47:14.020
You'll never hear me say an acronym. And I should have known that one.

47:14.520 --> 47:18.695
I'll say it. My question's actually, what happens to the bond

47:19.000 --> 47:22.757
should the state take over the road maintenance? They designated it as

47:22.869 --> 47:25.743
a state road. Do we continue to pay on the bond till the end of

47:26.243 --> 47:29.212
the bond? Yes, unless you can negotiate something with the state to pay you.

47:29.372 --> 47:32.423
I mean, never say never. I would ask,

47:32.487 --> 47:36.116
certainly, but my guess is that they would likely say, oh, we're taking the

47:36.616 --> 47:39.087
maintenance. Too bad, you paid for it. But never say never.

47:42.779 --> 47:47.803
If there aren't any other questions, I, I think— are we looking for specifically

47:47.916 --> 47:51.415
which of the 3 options we're comfortable with? That, that would be preferable

47:51.608 --> 47:54.802
because it allows us to— allows us— it allows him to

47:55.059 --> 47:58.334
write up the various documents. Yep, Mr. Larson will need to prepare the

47:58.834 --> 48:01.930
documents, and there are 3 different sets. If it's depending upon which option,

48:02.042 --> 48:05.349
it's best to have some consensus. I'll speak first, Mayor,

48:05.429 --> 48:07.982
if you'll let me. I think we should do the one where we, we We

48:08.482 --> 48:11.930
guarantee the security. I mean, we get a lower interest rate. I also am uncomfortable

48:12.430 --> 48:15.838
with setting aside $900,000 or $800,000 that sits in that

48:16.434 --> 48:20.286
revenue fund that sits on the front end. It reduces

48:20.399 --> 48:23.867
the amount of money that we can put to work,

48:24.662 --> 48:27.634
and that money becomes worth less and less as we have to sit on that

48:27.683 --> 48:31.157
for 15 years. So I just— The third option makes

48:31.657 --> 48:35.456
the most sense to me. It's certainly the the safest for the bondholders

48:35.504 --> 48:36.994
and probably the riskiest for the city.

48:39.682 --> 48:43.098
Sorry, but I also think that we get the best bang for our buck.

48:43.940 --> 48:46.395
Are you stating option 3 just for clarity? Okay.

48:48.882 --> 48:52.113
I'm in the same boat. I think that that puts us in the strongest

48:52.613 --> 48:55.528
position to be able to do all the things that we know we're gonna have

48:55.576 --> 48:58.943
to tackle in the next couple of years. And it makes

48:59.443 --> 49:03.342
sense to go for the lowest interest rate possible. So I'm in

49:03.842 --> 49:07.645
agreeing with Councilmember Wright. And I kind of heard whispers

49:07.741 --> 49:11.113
on this side that everybody says Option 3. So for sake of time, I think,

49:11.210 --> 49:13.987
uh, it looks like Option 3 is,

49:14.517 --> 49:18.339
is our preference. So is there any other needs

49:18.839 --> 49:22.144
on this specific item that you guys need, or— Nope. Thank you for your time.

49:22.305 --> 49:24.264
Appreciate it. Thank you. Thank you both.

49:26.062 --> 49:29.996
All right, moving right along. We will move to discussion item

49:30.496 --> 49:33.817
1C. This is about recreation events sponsorship and advertising.

49:34.556 --> 49:38.425
This, I know Steve Mumford has a little bit and also our Community Services Board

49:38.925 --> 49:42.199
Chair Jesse Southworth, and Melissa also wanted to

49:42.699 --> 49:45.089
lead in with a little bit. You're welcome to plug in for the presentation.

49:45.330 --> 49:48.124
While she's walking up here, I just wanted to let the rest of council know

49:48.624 --> 49:52.684
that I, first, I love being a liaison to this board. But second,

49:53.278 --> 49:56.907
Jesse and I had a really great opportunity to meet with Jason Hall and Mayor

49:57.372 --> 50:00.279
and just talk about the state of soccer and where we're headed and some of

50:00.779 --> 50:03.923
those things. And as a part of that conversation,

50:04.598 --> 50:07.921
making sure that we're competitive in what we offer

50:08.210 --> 50:11.486
our kids that sign up was a big part

50:11.986 --> 50:15.484
of the discussion. And as we got into that, we started talking about ways

50:15.516 --> 50:19.302
to pay for that and it naturally led to a conversation about

50:19.366 --> 50:21.578
sponsorships for soccer and just rec things.

50:22.155 --> 50:26.195
And I felt more comfortable bringing it back to the body so

50:26.695 --> 50:29.353
that everyone would have a chance to weigh in on it. And that's kind of

50:29.450 --> 50:32.768
where we are today. So— And then, Viss, before you get started,

50:32.816 --> 50:36.343
Steve, did you have anything that you wanted to lead in with? Just to note

50:36.843 --> 50:40.783
that Jason Hall and Dawn Hancock are here to answer questions.

50:40.912 --> 50:44.688
I know Jessie's going to— she's taking taken this on and is going to present

50:45.188 --> 50:48.378
and answer your questions and represent our board as

50:48.475 --> 50:51.987
well. But she'll talk about sports and

50:53.083 --> 50:55.918
events a little bit as well. So as you're thinking of sponsorships,

50:56.386 --> 51:00.200
we're thinking of the whole gamut. So yep,

51:01.638 --> 51:04.880
turn it over to Jessie. Thank you,

51:04.913 --> 51:09.027
one second, sorry. It's real

51:09.127 --> 51:09.509
weird.

51:14.210 --> 51:17.188
Okay, I don't know why.

51:20.670 --> 51:24.454
All right, good afternoon, Mayor and Council. Um, so the

51:24.954 --> 51:28.674
Community Services Board would like to explore sponsorship

51:29.174 --> 51:31.408
opportunities for recreation programs and community events.

51:32.321 --> 51:35.621
Uh, right now the city does not accept any sponsorships for

51:36.121 --> 51:39.263
recreation programs, but I know they've been used in the past for events,

51:39.344 --> 51:42.406
but nothing formal has ever been, uh,

51:42.487 --> 51:46.849
created or followed. Um, and I think that's just a missed opportunity,

51:46.930 --> 51:49.048
so I wanted to take this on. Um,

51:51.376 --> 51:55.435
today I'm not asking for any, like, official final approval

51:55.484 --> 51:59.285
of a sponsorship program. We just simply are looking for direction on whether

51:59.785 --> 52:04.445
or not the board should move forward with discussing and developing a formal sponsorship

52:04.558 --> 52:07.721
policy framework. If there is support, we would work

52:07.785 --> 52:10.527
through the details and bring a recommendation back to the council for review.

52:14.623 --> 52:18.238
I think why this is worth doing, sponsorships create

52:19.668 --> 52:23.283
a win-win-win situation without burdening families with higher fees or

52:23.783 --> 52:27.376
putting everything on taxpayers. Sponsorships would directly improve

52:27.410 --> 52:31.080
resident residents' experiences. They allow for lower costs to

52:31.128 --> 52:34.339
participate, better equipment and facilities, access to scholarships

52:34.839 --> 52:36.892
for those who need them, and overall higher quality programs.

52:37.968 --> 52:41.532
Local businesses want to support their community, and sponsorships give them

52:41.661 --> 52:45.482
an organized, meaningful channel to do that while building goodwill.

52:46.301 --> 52:50.540
And sponsorships would help stretch the city's budget further

52:50.556 --> 52:52.547
and reduce the burden of municipal funding.

52:54.330 --> 52:57.846
As you can see here, many of our neighboring cities

52:57.878 --> 53:01.170
already have sponsorship programs in place. They range anywhere

53:01.670 --> 53:05.344
from— most of them are around $300 to around

53:05.844 --> 53:09.326
$25,000. And that shows to me 2 things.

53:09.503 --> 53:13.132
First, there's a lot of flexibility in how a program can be structured. And second,

53:13.678 --> 53:17.772
there's real potential to improve the recreation program services and events provided

53:18.272 --> 53:21.571
for our residents. This is a closer

53:22.071 --> 53:25.660
look at what Saratoga Springs offers for their recreation sponsorships,

53:25.740 --> 53:29.937
and it would be a fairly simple plan

53:30.437 --> 53:33.292
for us to recreate. Each tier provides additional benefits,

53:33.340 --> 53:36.646
giving businesses more visibility and value at higher sponsorship levels.

53:39.920 --> 53:43.639
My personal belief is that we should start with the jersey

53:43.672 --> 53:46.945
logo sponsorships. They're simple, clear, and would require

53:47.445 --> 53:51.409
minimal effort to implement. These images show

53:51.425 --> 53:55.134
what leagues in our area are already doing, including the Eagle Mountain Youth

53:55.634 --> 53:58.570
Soccer, which is who we partnered with, one of the club teams,

53:58.635 --> 54:02.488
Liverpool, and even you've got Real Salt Lake there. The approach

54:02.988 --> 54:06.358
would help offset costs, allow us to provide higher quality

54:06.858 --> 54:10.099
materials, and improve the overall experience for our recreation participants.

54:11.632 --> 54:14.597
For event sponsorships, I pulled Lehigh as an example.

54:16.440 --> 54:19.808
This model provides an organized path with clear expectations for both the

54:20.308 --> 54:23.961
city and potential sponsors. We could do something similar or start

54:24.495 --> 54:26.225
even with a simpler version.

54:29.015 --> 54:30.877
And then the final question is,

54:32.560 --> 54:35.982
are you comfortable with the board continuing to explore the overall concept

54:35.999 --> 54:39.663
of the sponsorships? And more importantly, are there any concerns,

54:39.728 --> 54:42.728
limits, or red lines you would— we should be aware of before we go further?

54:46.120 --> 54:49.695
I'll add too really quick that we had the opportunity to meet with

54:50.195 --> 54:53.351
Jason. We haven't sat down with Dawn yet to find out what her desire and

54:53.851 --> 54:56.988
needs are, but that would be a part of the Community Services Board's process

54:57.583 --> 55:02.311
before they did anything to really get feedback

55:02.408 --> 55:07.222
from events. I personally

55:07.722 --> 55:11.116
think this is a great way to lift some cost from the parents.

55:11.308 --> 55:14.850
That's the direction that we're going. Um, it also gives

55:15.350 --> 55:18.920
an opportunity for businesses to market throughout

55:19.420 --> 55:23.167
the community and contribute to the community maybe in other ways that they

55:23.667 --> 55:26.789
can't. So I, I am fine with it.

55:27.750 --> 55:31.645
The one thing that I'm just kind of thinking through what,

55:31.677 --> 55:35.060
what kind of events would we have sponsored. I, I, I think in

55:35.560 --> 55:38.202
some events it might be okay, in other events— and maybe Don could guide us

55:38.795 --> 55:41.295
on where we could do support as a city— but we also want to be

55:41.795 --> 55:44.966
careful to not, I guess, playing favorites. If you go up to

55:45.466 --> 55:49.118
Idaho Falls for the 4th of July, it's a Melaleuca fireworks

55:49.150 --> 55:52.853
show even though it's Idaho Falls. Right. So we just need to be careful to

55:53.142 --> 55:57.061
make sure that we're retaining the events that we want to support and farming

55:57.157 --> 56:01.325
out events that maybe we're okay with third parties paying

56:01.421 --> 56:03.770
for. But I love the idea of jerseys for sure.

56:06.748 --> 56:09.789
The thought that just kind of came to my mind along those lines is if

56:10.387 --> 56:14.091
you have a sponsorship partner that suddenly

56:14.123 --> 56:17.280
is somebody we don't want to be a partner with because something happens, we need

56:17.780 --> 56:22.339
to have the capability of being able to absorb whatever that sponsorship

56:22.839 --> 56:26.090
So to his point about a fireworks show, let's say that our sponsor, whoever that

56:26.590 --> 56:30.315
might be, suddenly has done something in the media, everybody hates them. Right.

56:31.359 --> 56:33.529
We need to be able to have the capability of being able to pull out

56:34.029 --> 56:37.370
of something like that. Second thing, love the idea with

56:37.870 --> 56:41.673
the jerseys. I think that's smart. I'd say let's go even further

56:41.899 --> 56:45.190
and, and go after the field markers, all of that kind

56:45.690 --> 56:49.335
of things, things that There's a cost associated with that we could do better with

56:49.835 --> 56:53.027
and have better ways of marking things. I think those are great

56:53.527 --> 56:56.770
things. I do think there's a maybe an argument to be made as separating them

56:57.255 --> 57:00.795
so that there are sponsorships for recreation and sponsorships for events,

57:03.641 --> 57:07.656
and depending on the event, I mean, obviously with like some

57:07.688 --> 57:11.123
of our big events, maybe we want to structure that differently, right?

57:11.559 --> 57:15.001
To be able to. get a sponsorship goal that we need to

57:15.501 --> 57:19.266
