02:21:44.854 --> 02:21:47.436
after kid after kid, I was like, oh, I know that kid. I used to

02:21:47.936 --> 02:21:50.774
be like their scout leader, you know, different things like that. And I was thinking

02:21:51.274 --> 02:21:55.831
to myself, These kids grew up here. They started

02:21:56.331 --> 02:22:00.263
kindergarten here in Eagle Mountain, and now they're graduating from our high school.

02:22:00.889 --> 02:22:04.518
And it just really hit home with me how

02:22:05.866 --> 02:22:09.880
the magnitude of the decisions that we make here at City Council

02:22:10.378 --> 02:22:13.798
and how much those decisions impact the kids

02:22:14.376 --> 02:22:17.619
and the people of our community. And I was thinking

02:22:18.119 --> 02:22:21.903
about the fact that we're not just passing budgets, But we're creating a place

02:22:21.951 --> 02:22:25.895
our children can be proud to call their hometown. And whether they

02:22:25.943 --> 02:22:30.223
wear the colors of Cedar Valley High School or Westlake High School or

02:22:30.336 --> 02:22:33.991
Rockwell High School, they have one thing in common:

02:22:34.279 --> 02:22:37.678
that this place is theirs. And I

02:22:37.790 --> 02:22:42.295
hope that as they go out into the real world and take

02:22:42.795 --> 02:22:46.115
on the next big adventure, that they are proud to call this place. Place home

02:22:46.293 --> 02:22:50.065
and that we can do them justice by the things that

02:22:50.114 --> 02:22:51.555
we do here on City Council.

02:22:54.870 --> 02:22:58.065
Thanks, Mayor. Welcome everyone, it's good to see you. Um,

02:22:58.275 --> 02:23:02.358
thank you to the planning commissioners who have, who have now officially been appointed

02:23:02.390 --> 02:23:05.676
and sworn in. Welcome to the fight. You'll know what that

02:23:06.176 --> 02:23:09.231
means here shortly. I know, I know Chad's been involved in that, uh, in that

02:23:09.731 --> 02:23:13.278
battle already, and we're just really grateful for you. Um, Pony Express

02:23:13.778 --> 02:23:16.167
Days— I always love Pony Express Days, and I'm excited for that. The 4th of

02:23:16.667 --> 02:23:19.859
July in particular always makes me smile. I had the

02:23:20.359 --> 02:23:23.309
chance over the last 2 days to— in fact, this afternoon I, I went to

02:23:23.809 --> 02:23:27.530
one. But I, I have 2 of my grandchildren. I have a grandchild who's graduating

02:23:27.547 --> 02:23:30.436
from 6th grade and getting ready to go into the new middle school,

02:23:31.399 --> 02:23:34.609
and, uh, not the new one, but the one here in City Center. And so

02:23:35.109 --> 02:23:39.275
I've been to Mountain Trails Elementary And it's

02:23:39.775 --> 02:23:43.137
a pretty interesting thing to be a public servant on the City Council and to

02:23:43.637 --> 02:23:46.486
spend time in the community at some of the community events and at a school

02:23:46.986 --> 02:23:50.637
in particular. Boy, there's just a truckload of kids and parents and

02:23:50.717 --> 02:23:54.114
families and grandparents. And I'm just,

02:23:54.402 --> 02:23:57.847
I'm just really appreciative of the teachers, the staff that work

02:23:58.347 --> 02:24:01.950
there, and the tremendous work that they're doing with our students and with our kids.

02:24:02.030 --> 02:24:05.942
And today I was, I was John Hancock. I've got a grandson who just

02:24:06.442 --> 02:24:10.496
graduated from kindergarten, and I don't remember doing kindergarten

02:24:10.528 --> 02:24:14.057
graduation when I was in kindergarten, or 6th grade graduation for that matter.

02:24:14.939 --> 02:24:18.707
But I, I, my mind went back

02:24:19.207 --> 02:24:22.684
as I sat there and listened to the things that he recited.

02:24:23.181 --> 02:24:25.924
They had the Pledge of Allegiance. They have some things that they say in the

02:24:26.424 --> 02:24:29.484
morning. And, and anyway, I was really moved by

02:24:29.984 --> 02:24:33.867
by what they're being taught in those schools. And what

02:24:34.367 --> 02:24:37.753
I remembered was going back to a council meeting where I

02:24:37.785 --> 02:24:41.334
was really wrestling with whether or not John Hancock Charter School

02:24:41.834 --> 02:24:44.754
should be placed where it's at. I'm still not sure it's in the right location.

02:24:44.802 --> 02:24:48.704
I'm going to say that just to be official. But the fact

02:24:49.204 --> 02:24:51.787
that we were able to get to a yes, and I really wrestled with getting

02:24:51.851 --> 02:24:54.741
to that yes, and I know that other council members did as well.

02:24:55.928 --> 02:24:58.607
But you fast forward a few years and you start to look at those impacts.

02:24:58.671 --> 02:25:01.077
Those are the types of things that we're building. Those are the types of things

02:25:01.577 --> 02:25:05.328
that you're building as community members. So thank you for your energy and your effort,

02:25:05.392 --> 02:25:08.809
for your dedication, for your patience. Traffic is awful.

02:25:09.082 --> 02:25:12.771
We know that. We're fighting the fight. Some days it

02:25:13.271 --> 02:25:16.637
feels like we're going backwards, but most days we're actually making a small dent in

02:25:17.137 --> 02:25:20.086
it and trying to, trying to move it in the right direction. I just love

02:25:20.586 --> 02:25:24.563
Eagle Mountain. I love the people of Eagle Mountain. I love this

02:25:24.741 --> 02:25:28.768
community, which is a very unique and robust

02:25:29.431 --> 02:25:33.174
and a unique destination for families to come and

02:25:33.674 --> 02:25:36.978
to live in a family-centric community. And I'm just grateful to each of you for

02:25:37.478 --> 02:25:37.807
making it that.

02:25:44.441 --> 02:25:48.069
2 days ago, there was an amazing rededication

02:25:49.459 --> 02:25:52.929
of this country as a dedicated

02:25:53.429 --> 02:25:56.769
land for, for God and God's purposes. And if

02:25:57.269 --> 02:26:00.513
you did not listen to Speaker Mike Johnson's prayer that

02:26:00.561 --> 02:26:03.758
he offered to rededicate this country, I encourage you to

02:26:04.258 --> 02:26:07.470
look that up on YouTube and listen to that. It's about 10 minutes.

02:26:08.434 --> 02:26:11.518
It will move you to tears and also remind us

02:26:12.113 --> 02:26:14.828
who the founder of this country really is.

02:26:15.329 --> 02:26:19.026
one nation under God. God set

02:26:19.526 --> 02:26:23.451
up this country. He preserved it for numerous— like,

02:26:23.499 --> 02:26:26.996
he preserved it for millennia for us

02:26:27.496 --> 02:26:30.870
to now occupy it. And you got to think, well, if it was preserved for

02:26:31.370 --> 02:26:33.870
that long, for what reasons did he preserve it?

02:26:35.145 --> 02:26:39.544
And I would invite you just to take

02:26:39.691 --> 02:26:43.059
the 10 minutes to listen to Mike Johnson's

02:26:43.559 --> 02:26:47.395
prayer, Speaker Johnson's prayer, and then take another 10 minutes to share

02:26:47.895 --> 02:26:50.975
with your family. It is very powerful and very moving.

02:26:51.264 --> 02:26:55.728
I'll also read the scripture that President

02:26:56.228 --> 02:26:59.244
Trump shared. He said,

02:26:59.421 --> 02:27:02.745
and this is God talking about

02:27:02.969 --> 02:27:07.577
the dedication of the Temple of Solomon that was God's

02:27:07.625 --> 02:27:11.365
temple. If my people which are called by my name shall humble

02:27:11.397 --> 02:27:14.671
themselves and pray and seek my face and turn from

02:27:14.687 --> 02:27:17.961
their wicked ways, then will I hear from heaven and

02:27:18.121 --> 02:27:22.181
will forgive their sins and will heal their land. And I

02:27:22.534 --> 02:27:25.696
invite us to remember the,

02:27:25.824 --> 02:27:29.500
the real founding father of this country, and then the founding fathers

02:27:29.772 --> 02:27:32.661
and mothers that were inspired to help set this up.

02:27:33.592 --> 02:27:37.410
I invite us to share these messages with our family so that they will remember.

02:27:38.057 --> 02:27:41.341
And it really is about that. It's so easy in our day and age of

02:27:41.374 --> 02:27:44.744
materialism to forget who set

02:27:45.244 --> 02:27:48.643
this country up and for what purposes. And so I give

02:27:49.143 --> 02:27:49.159
you that invitation.

02:27:54.714 --> 02:27:58.164
Love seeing everybody here. I appreciate those who are here. I wish the

02:27:58.664 --> 02:28:01.688
people from Silver Lake would have stayed. Um, I live there,

02:28:01.833 --> 02:28:05.188
by the way. I'm very aware of the situation there. My kids

02:28:05.204 --> 02:28:07.805
go to Brookhaven, and next year they'll go to Silver Lake.

02:28:08.656 --> 02:28:11.947
Um, one of the things I think is important, and I want to

02:28:11.963 --> 02:28:14.340
highlight a couple things— I was grateful that Jared,

02:28:15.239 --> 02:28:18.867
our mayor, decided to meet with ABC and to kind of lay

02:28:19.367 --> 02:28:22.560
out what we're trying to accomplish. One of the disappointing things I'm going to say

02:28:23.041 --> 02:28:28.193
is that people jump so quickly to conclusions of nefariousness

02:28:28.803 --> 02:28:32.300
about the people who are up here. And I want to dispel

02:28:32.412 --> 02:28:36.053
that first and foremost. Nobody's benefiting financially

02:28:36.553 --> 02:28:39.342
or otherwise by being here. In fact, it's the opposite.

02:28:40.208 --> 02:28:43.545
The sacrifices that are made by the people who are your elected officials

02:28:43.657 --> 02:28:47.667
is substantial, financially and otherwise, to be

02:28:47.731 --> 02:28:49.720
here to represent the people of Eagle Mountain.

02:28:51.036 --> 02:28:53.217
With regards to the discussions about roads,

02:28:54.310 --> 02:28:58.021
There's no greater advocates for getting the roads fixed than the five people sitting

02:28:58.521 --> 02:29:01.877
up here and our mayor. The reason Lake Mountain Corridor

02:29:02.377 --> 02:29:05.364
even exists is because of the people sitting here,

02:29:06.440 --> 02:29:08.931
and the fact that it's going to take eight to ten years has nothing to

02:29:09.431 --> 02:29:12.947
do with us. That has to do with finances. I think it's funny

02:29:13.447 --> 02:29:15.358
when people are like, "We'll just spend the money." You just, you know, take it

02:29:15.858 --> 02:29:18.426
from here and put it here kind of a thing. And I think I would

02:29:18.926 --> 02:29:22.666
encourage people to learn. Before we make statements like that, understand where the

02:29:23.166 --> 02:29:26.711
money comes from, that there are limitations to how money can be

02:29:27.211 --> 02:29:30.292
spent. And trust me, if there was a big pot of money that we could

02:29:30.308 --> 02:29:34.482
take and build the road, it would already be built. We benefit individually,

02:29:34.947 --> 02:29:38.094
our families benefit from having roads that get us in and out of the city.

02:29:38.817 --> 02:29:41.851
And even to the, to the comments from the people who live in Silver

02:29:42.351 --> 02:29:45.640
Lake with me, believe me, we want to have connectivity, but what we

02:29:46.140 --> 02:29:48.531
don't want to do do is create problems that are bigger than what we are

02:29:49.031 --> 02:29:52.514
already experiencing. That's the reason that we have the studies being done,

02:29:53.093 --> 02:29:55.582
and we have to look at everything as a holistic picture,

02:29:56.064 --> 02:29:59.100
from what Saratoga Springs is planning to do to the south of us,

02:30:00.096 --> 02:30:03.276
whether or not that connection is the right connection, or if there are other connections.

02:30:03.790 --> 02:30:07.533
We have to make those contemplative decisions, because if we don't

02:30:07.549 --> 02:30:10.906
and everything's a knee-jerk and we act in the very moment because something

02:30:10.938 --> 02:30:14.375
needs to be done right this moment, we set future generations up for

02:30:14.391 --> 02:30:18.074
consequences that we could have avoided. And I just think it's important

02:30:18.574 --> 02:30:21.716
for people to understand that we don't take these decisions lightly. We argue

02:30:21.893 --> 02:30:25.484
a lot about these decisions in closed

02:30:25.775 --> 02:30:29.527
doors, in one-on-ones, oftentimes to

02:30:29.592 --> 02:30:32.773
get to the solution that is best. And if you think for a second that

02:30:32.822 --> 02:30:36.123
we're, you know, just making these decisions lightly, it's— I'm going to

02:30:36.172 --> 02:30:40.360
tell you, it's not. A lot of sleep has been lost. A lot of tempers

02:30:40.424 --> 02:30:43.917
have flared, trying to decide how the best way to accomplish

02:30:43.966 --> 02:30:47.249
these things for the for the citizens. And I just think it's so important to

02:30:47.281 --> 02:30:50.436
highlight the work that is being done by the people

02:30:50.936 --> 02:30:54.011
that you've elected. You can clear us all out in the next election if you

02:30:54.511 --> 02:30:56.276
think that'll solve it, but I'm going to tell you that would be a mistake.

02:30:57.004 --> 02:31:00.015
The people who are fighting for you right now is the best council that has

02:31:00.242 --> 02:31:04.096
been in Eagle Mountain. I've been here thirteen years. They're pointed in the same direction,

02:31:04.802 --> 02:31:08.142
and that is progress. We now have allies at

02:31:08.575 --> 02:31:11.851
our county. We have allies in our surrounding cities.

02:31:12.204 --> 02:31:15.078
We have allies at the state. That has not been the case ever,

02:31:15.961 --> 02:31:19.638
and that's because there are individuals working collectively

02:31:19.654 --> 02:31:22.608
for the same goals to try to make Eagle Mountain a safer place,

02:31:23.748 --> 02:31:26.590
to get better egress and ingress into the city. And I just, I want to

02:31:27.090 --> 02:31:28.983
say that. I want to be on the record saying that whoever wants to take

02:31:29.483 --> 02:31:32.179
that and do whatever they want with it. I'm available for you to call if

02:31:32.679 --> 02:31:36.130
you think that I'm crazy. Call me and tell me I'm crazy, but let's have

02:31:36.630 --> 02:31:39.986
a one-on-one conversation. I'll produce the facts for you of why we make the decisions

02:31:40.486 --> 02:31:42.877
that we make. Maybe that'll give you a better understanding of why we make the

02:31:43.377 --> 02:31:46.427
decisions that we make. But I think we're all open to that very same thing.

02:31:46.475 --> 02:31:49.896
Our telephone numbers are available. I'd rather you call me

02:31:50.009 --> 02:31:53.558
than try to fight this out on Facebook because it doesn't seem like anybody's interested

02:31:54.058 --> 02:31:56.659
in the truth there. So give me a call and I'll tell you the facts

02:31:57.159 --> 02:31:59.751
that I know and why I make the decisions that I make. Thank you for

02:32:00.251 --> 02:32:03.051
coming and being a part of the process. Those that are watching online,

02:32:03.490 --> 02:32:06.584
get a hold of us. Then you don't have to wonder anymore. You can know

02:32:07.084 --> 02:32:09.024
the truth about why we make the decisions. Thanks.

02:32:11.585 --> 02:32:14.872
Thank you, fellow council members, and thank you everyone for coming

02:32:14.904 --> 02:32:17.998
tonight for whatever purpose you're here— to learn, to observe.

02:32:18.796 --> 02:32:22.460
I wanted to share a quote from a book that I read. It's by

02:32:22.556 --> 02:32:26.157
Walter Isaacson. He's one of the great biographers

02:32:26.205 --> 02:32:29.387
of our time. He's done biographies on Steve Jobs,

02:32:30.004 --> 02:32:33.416
Elon Musk. He did one

02:32:33.594 --> 02:32:38.029
on Michelangelo and—

02:32:39.599 --> 02:32:41.638
was it Michelangelo? I think it was.

02:32:42.595 --> 02:32:46.437
No, it was Leonardo. Sorry. And he did one on Benjamin Franklin.

02:32:46.486 --> 02:32:49.534
And I wanted to share just a quick

02:32:50.034 --> 02:32:53.056
quote that Benjamin Franklin said, that knowledge is obtained by the use of the ear

02:32:53.665 --> 02:32:57.226
than of the tongue. And that's a good reminder.

02:32:57.498 --> 02:33:01.459
We have been going on these listening tours to each neighborhood

02:33:01.959 --> 02:33:05.821
around Eagle Mountain. I think I've been to 5 now. Somehow I've been able to

02:33:05.837 --> 02:33:09.462
go to all of them with my fellow council members. And it's been a

02:33:09.962 --> 02:33:13.840
really eye-opening experience to go to each community and just hear what the needs

02:33:13.856 --> 02:33:17.790
are. Silver Lake wanting to connect to the high school and have

02:33:18.290 --> 02:33:22.430
access to a road. Here recently we had one in Cason,

02:33:22.686 --> 02:33:26.604
correct? Cason's neighborhood in Highlands on

02:33:26.700 --> 02:33:30.730
Ranches Parkway. And I would just say that oftentimes

02:33:31.230 --> 02:33:34.744
if we will just listen and ask questions, we become much better

02:33:35.244 --> 02:33:39.849
informed, and that's really our goal with those. So as we celebrate our 250th,

02:33:40.524 --> 02:33:43.783
in the world that we live in, I think it's a good practice for us

02:33:43.879 --> 02:33:47.669
to learn to listen. As my mom would say, Zach, God gave you

02:33:48.169 --> 02:33:51.587
2 ears and 1 mouth. And she was right.

02:33:51.699 --> 02:33:54.332
So I'll go ahead and close with that. But I appreciate all of you being

02:33:54.832 --> 02:33:58.186
here. Okay. Thank you. I just want to add a couple of

02:33:58.686 --> 02:34:02.328
comments similar to what Councilmember Wood was touching

02:34:02.345 --> 02:34:06.024
on. But on Thursday, I was sitting at a MAG meeting.

02:34:07.733 --> 02:34:11.118
I think I was at roughly hour 17 or

02:34:11.618 --> 02:34:15.027
18 on my day that day. Late— those, those meetings go late,

02:34:15.043 --> 02:34:18.295
and I started UFA extra early. Literally looking

02:34:18.795 --> 02:34:22.836
at the prioritization of our funding that some of our roads

02:34:22.932 --> 02:34:26.803
in Eagle Mountain did not score high enough on to make the funding threshold.

02:34:28.240 --> 02:34:31.408
I was less than pleased for

02:34:31.908 --> 02:34:34.205
your enjoyment. And I let them know,

02:34:34.992 --> 02:34:38.078
and I will guarantee you that I'm advocating for Eagle Mountain roads.

02:34:38.303 --> 02:34:41.702
I think because of my just short of a tantrum

02:34:42.235 --> 02:34:44.932
that some of our roads might get moved up into a funding category.

02:34:45.497 --> 02:34:50.138
So I think to the ABC news

02:34:50.235 --> 02:34:53.978
piece that ran yesterday, they did about a 5-minute interview

02:34:54.464 --> 02:34:58.348
and picked about a 10-second clip. About 8

02:34:58.364 --> 02:35:01.048
to 10 years. I didn't say that I was okay with 8 to 10 years.

02:35:01.097 --> 02:35:03.538
What I said is that's not soon enough.

02:35:05.098 --> 02:35:08.794
So I haven't had time to look, but I've heard that on Facebook,

02:35:08.859 --> 02:35:12.232
you know, I'm not doing enough. I literally went on the news

02:35:12.329 --> 02:35:16.129
to say we're not waiting 8 to 10 years. We're going to take

02:35:16.629 --> 02:35:20.323
action. So just kind of an update that those things are happening.

02:35:21.149 --> 02:35:25.449
All weekend I've been working on our roads. I've called the state leadership

02:35:25.949 --> 02:35:30.102
to request a meeting. We're going to do our best to work

02:35:30.602 --> 02:35:33.760
with them. They've invested a lot of money in our area. But we have to

02:35:33.776 --> 02:35:36.984
have a resolution much sooner than what it

02:35:37.484 --> 02:35:40.738
is right now. So I just wanted to report on that. As I mentioned,

02:35:40.770 --> 02:35:44.268
we're sitting in MAG as within a 20-minute period—

02:35:44.621 --> 02:35:47.829
and this I'm going to expand on— we had 5 calls within

02:35:47.910 --> 02:35:51.574
20 minutes right behind 3 or 4 calls that had already happened,

02:35:53.280 --> 02:35:56.504
with agencies responding as far as Pleasant Grove, American Fork,

02:35:56.553 --> 02:36:00.301
Lehi with mutual aid. 5 calls within 20

02:36:00.801 --> 02:36:04.279
minutes. Extrications, life flights,

02:36:05.249 --> 02:36:09.491
search and rescue, not minor fender benders. And we

02:36:09.991 --> 02:36:13.208
needed help from across the county. We can't do this any

02:36:13.224 --> 02:36:16.603
longer. So With that, a huge appreciation, and I

02:36:17.103 --> 02:36:20.524
know— I don't even know what cruise we're on, but all of UFA, all of

02:36:21.024 --> 02:36:24.766
our first responders that are keeping us safe even

02:36:24.782 --> 02:36:28.406
throughout the county, that we sure appreciate the effort that's put

02:36:28.906 --> 02:36:31.981
in to keep us moving, even if it is at a slow pace right now.

02:36:32.432 --> 02:36:35.710
And we're doing our best to

02:36:36.210 --> 02:36:39.560
make sure that we can accelerate that timeline, and I'm not taking 8 years

02:36:39.810 --> 02:36:43.529
for an answer. That will not work. So we will

02:36:44.112 --> 02:36:48.010
take measures. I've been meeting with our attorney internally to see

02:36:48.155 --> 02:36:51.354
what it is that we can do to best serve us. And we don't

02:36:51.854 --> 02:36:54.712
know what that is yet, but we're working on those solutions. And I can promise

02:36:55.212 --> 02:36:57.457
you it's not on deaf ears. I sit in this traffic too.

02:36:59.190 --> 02:37:02.285
Just want to, on a lighter note, spend some time— I love spending time in

02:37:02.785 --> 02:37:05.428
the schools. For years I got told I had never been inside a school.

02:37:05.520 --> 02:37:09.148
School back during those discussions. Today and

02:37:09.245 --> 02:37:12.408
last week, and I'm there all the time, I did a Shark Tank event with

02:37:12.440 --> 02:37:16.245
the junior high. Super fun. We'll see

02:37:16.745 --> 02:37:18.429
if we can get some of that out on social media. But I got a—

02:37:18.943 --> 02:37:20.966
I told him I was like Simon Cowell. I'm not gonna be nice to these

02:37:21.030 --> 02:37:25.076
kids. But I did buy 2 businesses, so I'm broke.

02:37:26.249 --> 02:37:29.396
They had great pitches and it was kind of a math project

02:37:29.896 --> 02:37:33.473
and we— they were doing bungee jumping Barbies, and it

02:37:33.973 --> 02:37:37.325
was fun. So I got to sit next to Julie King and of course banter

02:37:37.825 --> 02:37:41.209
with her like we always do. And, and I—

02:37:41.610 --> 02:37:44.194
now I'm gonna go back to a more serious note because Julie was upset.

02:37:44.804 --> 02:37:46.954
As I was leaving, one of the little kids came running up to me and

02:37:47.454 --> 02:37:51.448
asked for my autograph. And it was, it was actually quite— she's

02:37:51.948 --> 02:37:54.417
like, did you pay that kid? You set this up, something's going on. And then

02:37:54.917 --> 02:37:58.444
we had this cool text thread, and I just wanted to say echo

02:37:58.944 --> 02:38:01.299
kind of what my fellow council members said about the opportunity to serve at this

02:38:01.411 --> 02:38:05.228
time. I sent her a message after I was done teasing,

02:38:05.260 --> 02:38:08.708
and, you know, we always poke each other, and said, you know what, it's never

02:38:09.208 --> 02:38:12.348
lost on me that nobody's looking for Jared Gray's autograph, not one

02:38:12.461 --> 02:38:15.700
person. They're there looking for an autograph from the

02:38:16.200 --> 02:38:19.693
mayor of Eagle Mountain. And that to me is a

02:38:20.193 --> 02:38:24.094
big deal. To those kids, it's not— they're not looking for me. They're looking for

02:38:24.594 --> 02:38:27.637
the mantle and the responsibilities

02:38:27.669 --> 02:38:31.276
that we hold as elected officials. So that was pretty touching

02:38:31.309 --> 02:38:34.675
for me, and I think that's important that we remember. I say it all the

02:38:35.175 --> 02:38:38.484
time, it's not mine, it's just my turn. And it was quite an honor to

02:38:38.984 --> 02:38:42.459
be able to represent the city in signing autographs.

02:38:42.572 --> 02:38:45.825
So that was a lot of fun. But thanks.

02:38:45.906 --> 02:38:48.592
So we've got to move on. Enough of that.

02:38:49.056 --> 02:38:52.645
Let's, uh, go to item number 12. It's actually— actually,

02:38:52.901 --> 02:38:56.298
all the items are consent agenda. This is the next thing on our thing.

02:38:56.346 --> 02:38:59.487
And remember, there's some corrections on item 14, and I believe there was— I didn't

02:38:59.987 --> 02:39:02.739
write them down, but, uh, one item that needed to be pulled possibly.

02:39:03.412 --> 02:39:06.825
Okay, uh, would— I'd be happy to make a motion.

02:39:07.241 --> 02:39:10.766
Okay, Mayor, I move that the city council approve the consent

02:39:11.266 --> 02:39:15.413
agenda, um, updating a typo

02:39:15.573 --> 02:39:19.719
in item 14A to state 2026

02:39:19.848 --> 02:39:24.389
through 2027 and removing 14B

02:39:25.001 --> 02:39:29.188
and placing it on the regularly scheduled items part of the agenda.

02:39:30.541 --> 02:39:33.374
Second. Yeah, I think I got, uh,

02:39:33.519 --> 02:39:36.112
Council Member Wright. So we got a motion from Council Member Clark and a second

02:39:36.612 --> 02:39:39.983
from Council Member Wright, and we'll start the vote with Council Member

02:39:40.483 --> 02:39:44.580
Whiting. Yes. Council Member Wood. Yes. Councilmember Huish?

02:39:45.014 --> 02:39:47.552
Yes. Councilmember Clark? Yes. Councilmember Wright? Yes.

02:39:48.403 --> 02:39:51.230
Okay, with that, we will— the consent agenda passes,

02:39:51.375 --> 02:39:55.568
and we will go to item 14B, which is— she

02:39:56.068 --> 02:39:59.327
said B. Okay, it's a

02:39:59.568 --> 02:40:03.182
resolution of Eagle Mountain City approving the memorandum of agreement with

02:40:03.246 --> 02:40:06.154
Ivory Homes. Did I— yes, Brad stuck around.

02:40:06.716 --> 02:40:10.330
So we went over this in the— at length in the

02:40:11.965 --> 02:40:15.845
Work session. Do you, from the council's perspective, do we need Brad

02:40:16.345 --> 02:40:18.539
up here? Do you need information from Marcus?

02:40:19.752 --> 02:40:22.223
What's the— what are the questions?

02:40:23.774 --> 02:40:25.939
Brad, you're welcome to come forward. I'm sure you're going to get asked questions.

02:40:27.687 --> 02:40:29.874
I didn't know what they wanted, but I might as well just call you up.

02:40:30.372 --> 02:40:33.796
So this is a memorandum that we've discussed at

02:40:34.296 --> 02:40:38.069
length that had to do with the road exchange and Yeah, it's, uh, in relation

02:40:38.182 --> 02:40:41.382
to the bypass road. There's, you know, various provisions

02:40:41.882 --> 02:40:45.241
of sort of understandings that the parties have as to how we

02:40:45.258 --> 02:40:48.863
want to proceed in the future on things and, uh, and what direction we

02:40:49.363 --> 02:40:52.581
want to go. And so this would be, you know, an agreement to work

02:40:53.081 --> 02:40:55.688
in good faith towards getting, you know, formalizing those issues.

02:40:59.912 --> 02:41:02.978
I think I expressed earlier, and this is not a question but an expression,

02:41:03.076 --> 02:41:06.298
I, I don't feel comfortable removing Citla from the agreement

02:41:07.197 --> 02:41:10.666
based on the— So we, you know, after the

02:41:11.388 --> 02:41:15.017
work session, I briefly spoke with Brad about the issue of the school

02:41:15.081 --> 02:41:18.566
site, and he mentioned that they can just unilaterally purchase that

02:41:18.614 --> 02:41:22.451
35-acre property from Citla per their agreement and

02:41:22.467 --> 02:41:26.401
do a deal with the city directly on that. And so the memorandum— When

02:41:26.465 --> 02:41:30.431
you say they, you mean Ivory? Ivory. Ivory could purchase from Sitla and then do

02:41:30.931 --> 02:41:33.948
an exchange with the city. So, so maybe I could— I mean, chime in a

02:41:34.448 --> 02:41:37.882
little bit. So maybe a part of the agreement is helpful. Yeah,

02:41:38.460 --> 02:41:41.093
so our agreement is a lease agreement with Sitla.

02:41:42.040 --> 02:41:45.637
The, um, Villages 1 through

02:41:45.733 --> 02:41:48.800
8 are currently part of our lease agreement.

02:41:49.828 --> 02:41:53.729
Um, any transaction, um, that we do in those 8

02:41:53.762 --> 02:41:57.248
villages We conduct the business

02:41:57.617 --> 02:42:01.200
and we purchase that from SITLA, and then

02:42:02.759 --> 02:42:06.722
either we give them a percentage of the residential

02:42:07.222 --> 02:42:10.967
sale, or in commercial uses, we split profits

02:42:11.080 --> 02:42:14.152
one way or the other. And so

02:42:14.732 --> 02:42:18.660
all the transactions happen through Ivory. So anything we do

02:42:18.773 --> 02:42:22.301
with you on this, We would do the transaction,

02:42:22.509 --> 02:42:26.116
and then we would get consent from Sitla before

02:42:26.616 --> 02:42:30.380
we proceed with anything. If that makes sense, the Pony Express

02:42:30.880 --> 02:42:34.628
parcel where we've talked about placing a

02:42:35.128 --> 02:42:38.748
potential school site is an option parcel in our

02:42:38.876 --> 02:42:42.531
lease agreement. So in order for us to work

02:42:42.723 --> 02:42:45.897
with you on that site, we would have to

02:42:45.913 --> 02:42:49.684
exercise our option to. take that piece and then work

02:42:50.184 --> 02:42:53.955
with Sitla to purchase it and then do all of the other

02:42:54.455 --> 02:42:58.017
horse trading ideas that we come up with. And that,

02:42:58.290 --> 02:43:02.112
that is the purpose of this memorandum of understanding, is that

02:43:02.561 --> 02:43:06.286
nothing's set in stone yet, but we will work towards

02:43:06.479 --> 02:43:10.060
a solution that's equitable for everyone, meaning Ivory,

02:43:10.188 --> 02:43:13.480
Sitla, and Eagle Mountain City. Thank you. Does that make sense?

02:43:16.550 --> 02:43:18.595
So would it make you more comfortable— I don't know if this is even a

02:43:19.095 --> 02:43:22.475
question— if they instead— it's a memorandum of understanding or of

02:43:22.975 --> 02:43:26.107
agreement, right? Um, does it make you more comfortable if

02:43:26.607 --> 02:43:29.932
it says they will? Right now it's kind of more of a good faith,

02:43:29.964 --> 02:43:34.319
so I'm trying to get that a little stronger, that they will make that transaction

02:43:34.819 --> 02:43:37.028
happen or facilitate that. Like, is it a verbiage thing?

02:43:38.496 --> 02:43:42.009
No, I was— if CITLA had any obligation,

02:43:42.426 --> 02:43:46.340
I didn't want that obligation removed. But if Ivory has a unilateral ability to

02:43:46.372 --> 02:43:49.660
execute on CITLA's portion of the agreement,

02:43:49.724 --> 02:43:53.060
then I feel better about that. Yeah, any agreement

02:43:53.109 --> 02:43:56.317
we do in the future, we will have to get their consent on.

02:43:56.942 --> 02:43:59.958
So we will work with you to come

02:44:00.006 --> 02:44:02.621
up with the levers that we want to pull,

02:44:03.374 --> 02:44:06.715
and then we will go take it to CITLA and get their Blessing

02:44:07.157 --> 02:44:08.760
before we proceed on anything.

02:44:11.507 --> 02:44:15.098
I've been pretty open about what I want removed. I don't want an

02:44:15.404 --> 02:44:18.704
MOA that deals with water reduction requirements.

02:44:19.155 --> 02:44:22.315
That doesn't mean that we can't negotiate that as part of an MTA agreement

02:44:22.815 --> 02:44:24.819
or an amendment. I don't want to be obligated to it, though.

02:44:29.659 --> 02:44:33.084
And I don't think this agreement does that. In my opinion, it was just a

02:44:33.584 --> 02:44:35.889
list of— Then it doesn't matter if it's in there. That's, that's kind of my

02:44:36.389 --> 02:44:39.512
point. If it doesn't matter, then it shouldn't be in there. I think it was

02:44:39.576 --> 02:44:42.750
just nothing, nothing's in there that's concrete

02:44:42.766 --> 02:44:46.630
though, right? These were just ideas that we could look

02:44:47.130 --> 02:44:49.756
at to, in trade for the school site.

02:44:51.118 --> 02:44:55.334
They weren't, they weren't concrete items. They were just potential things

02:44:55.350 --> 02:44:58.496
to look at. That's my only— Brad, if take

02:44:58.996 --> 02:45:00.293
that out of there, I don't have a problem with the rest of it.

02:45:00.374 --> 02:45:03.757
I think we can act in good faith with all the other things, but water

02:45:04.257 --> 02:45:06.525
is not one of those things I'm ready— I, I'm willing to horse trade with.

02:45:12.280 --> 02:45:15.410
I was just thinking how in a lot of ways the things that are in

02:45:15.668 --> 02:45:19.314
this memorandum of agreement are very similar to the

02:45:19.814 --> 02:45:23.452
tools that we put in our toolbox that we can use with the wildlife

02:45:23.952 --> 02:45:27.502
corridor. Not everybody's going to get all the things that we listed

02:45:27.614 --> 02:45:30.691
in the code, that this could be a tool. But it's,

02:45:31.316 --> 02:45:34.841
it's one of those things that a council has the ability to

02:45:34.906 --> 02:45:38.159
use to get good outcomes.

02:45:38.735 --> 02:45:42.004
But I am super sensitive to Council Member Woods'

02:45:42.661 --> 02:45:46.251
concerns. I'm just wondering if there's some language

02:45:46.363 --> 02:45:50.033
that we could change in the memorandum to make it so

02:45:50.533 --> 02:45:54.914
that it's clear that it's just Just a potential tool and not a guaranteed

02:45:54.946 --> 02:45:58.594
thing. I know that water's such a huge thing now,

02:45:58.883 --> 02:46:02.162
and every developer on the planet is like, you need to do

02:46:02.178 --> 02:46:05.906
this. So it's, it's hard when we do it

02:46:06.195 --> 02:46:09.923
in good faith with one, because then the expectations are that

02:46:09.971 --> 02:46:13.361
we will be able to do that with all the others, and it really

02:46:13.474 --> 02:46:17.203
is very case by case. So I'm struggling with

02:46:17.703 --> 02:46:17.738
that as well.

02:46:21.591 --> 02:46:25.319
Would it make any difference to, to you 2 council members if

02:46:25.400 --> 02:46:28.973
in, in E where it says city's considerations, we were

02:46:29.473 --> 02:46:32.993
to modify the language? It says the city will consider providing benefits to the developer,

02:46:33.073 --> 02:46:35.915
which may include, and then it lists some items there.

02:46:36.883 --> 02:46:40.497
If that were to change to the city may consider providing

02:46:40.595 --> 02:46:43.695
benefits to the developer, which may include— what

02:46:44.195 --> 02:46:47.639
if we remove the word will? Okay. Because will to me is a strong word.

02:46:47.768 --> 02:46:51.123
It says we're going to do that. May just

02:46:51.509 --> 02:46:54.303
opens it up to the fact that it's just a possibility. And I don't know

02:46:54.319 --> 02:46:56.631
how that would sit with you either, so I'm going to throw that out there

02:46:57.131 --> 02:46:59.810
and see how the council feels about it. And then I'll ask— I think the

02:47:00.310 --> 02:47:03.953
main thing is, is that's something you want to negotiate. For me,

02:47:03.969 --> 02:47:07.517
it's— that's not a tool. The other things, I think we can talk about any

02:47:08.017 --> 02:47:11.098
of those other things, but water's not on the table for me. And I think

02:47:11.598 --> 02:47:14.938
if we want to talk about that under different circumstances as

02:47:14.970 --> 02:47:18.329
part of a master development agreement or an amendment to a master development agreement,

02:47:18.730 --> 02:47:22.065
that's different. But I think putting that out there and saying that that's

02:47:22.228 --> 02:47:24.749
a negotiating tool that we're willing to do is the wrong message.

02:47:26.860 --> 02:47:30.470
So for me, it would be simply just removing EI

02:47:31.469 --> 02:47:34.403
from that. I'm fine with the rest. How do you feel about that, Mr.

02:47:34.451 --> 02:47:38.378
Mackey? To me, it's all a negotiation,

02:47:38.635 --> 02:47:42.376
right? That's the whole point of this memorandum, is trying

02:47:42.424 --> 02:47:45.282
to find a way that we can come to a win-win solution.

02:47:45.539 --> 02:47:49.536
And so if that's not on the table, then there will be something

02:47:49.553 --> 02:47:52.587
else that has to be negotiated.

02:47:52.956 --> 02:47:56.312
So, so what I mean, you say, Councilmember Wood, is if,

02:47:56.456 --> 02:47:59.924
if I is removed, that then you're going to be okay with that? There's a

02:48:00.424 --> 02:48:03.826
lot of other things in there that they get a benefit

02:48:04.326 --> 02:48:06.141
out of that we don't even need to have a discussion about water. I mean,

02:48:06.575 --> 02:48:10.785
density transfer, increased density, I mean, all those things, those reduced setbacks,

02:48:11.171 --> 02:48:14.947
those are big asks already. So then what I'm going to suggest

02:48:15.447 --> 02:48:18.900
is that we change the city not will but may consider providing benefits

02:48:18.948 --> 02:48:22.869
to the developer, which may include removing I and

02:48:22.965 --> 02:48:26.420
just going forward with II from there forward,

02:48:26.436 --> 02:48:29.564
right? Is the council going to be okay with that?

02:48:29.869 --> 02:48:32.994
That seems fair. Yeah, I mean, there's a lot to— You okay with that,

02:48:33.010 --> 02:48:36.087
Brad? We'll work through it. We're going to have some negotiation.

02:48:36.215 --> 02:48:39.756
This is not done. Yeah, this is— again, this is a— it's

02:48:40.256 --> 02:48:43.554
a template to say we're— a template of, of something

02:48:43.586 --> 02:48:47.640
to start a negotiation to work on, on a solution

02:48:47.897 --> 02:48:51.054
that benefits everyone. Okay, then, Mayor,

02:48:51.118 --> 02:48:54.564
if we're ready, can we, can we move to a motion I'm happy

02:48:55.064 --> 02:48:58.349
to entertain a motion. All right, let me get there. You want me to make

02:48:58.849 --> 02:49:01.604
it since I made the last one? Go ahead, go ahead. I mean, I'm happy

02:49:02.104 --> 02:49:05.918
to do it. Mayor, I move that the City Council approve

02:49:06.896 --> 02:49:11.034
Item 14B, a resolution of Eagle Mountain City, Utah, approving a memorandum

02:49:11.082 --> 02:49:15.268
of agreement with Ivory Homes with an adjustment to

02:49:15.768 --> 02:49:19.664
Section E. The language will say city's consideration the

02:49:20.164 --> 02:49:22.804
city may consider providing benefits to the developer,

02:49:23.468 --> 02:49:27.272
which may include then omitting I and

02:49:27.772 --> 02:49:31.031
continuing with II. Second. All right,

02:49:31.047 --> 02:49:34.401
we have a motion by Councilmember Wood and a second by Councilmember Wright, and we'll

02:49:34.901 --> 02:49:37.518
start the vote with Councilmember Wood.

02:49:38.392 --> 02:49:41.809
Yes. Councilmember Huish. Yes. Councilmember Clark.

02:49:41.954 --> 02:49:44.496
Yes. Councilmember Wright. Yes. Councilmember Whiting. Yes.

02:49:45.194 --> 02:49:48.455
Okay, that concludes our consent agenda, and we'll move on to our scheduled item.

02:49:48.520 --> 02:49:49.055
Thank you, Brad.

02:49:52.533 --> 02:49:55.673
Um, our next item is item 15A. It's an ordinance, uh,

02:49:56.156 --> 02:49:59.248
uh, and a public— this is a public hearing. An ordinance of Eagle Mountain City,

02:49:59.280 --> 02:50:05.384
Utah, amending the Eagle Mountain Municipal Code Chapter 17.10.030

02:50:05.659 --> 02:50:09.669
Definitions and 17.25.040

02:50:09.847 --> 02:50:13.536
Residential Development Standards. And this will be presented by our

02:50:14.036 --> 02:50:15.127
long-range planner, Elizabeth Fuchs.

02:50:27.329 --> 02:50:30.801
Good evening, Mayor and Council. So first, I wanted

02:50:31.301 --> 02:50:35.049
to start out with explaining the genesis of this item.

02:50:35.776 --> 02:50:39.556
So a few months ago, we approved the WUI map,

02:50:39.572 --> 02:50:43.650
so the Wildland Urban Interface map. And in preparation for that,

02:50:43.955 --> 02:50:48.626
Brandon and I met with the fire officials and discussed different aspects

02:50:48.771 --> 02:50:52.784
of that map. And during that conversation, I asked

02:50:52.881 --> 02:50:56.637
if there was anything we could do as a city to improve

02:50:57.247 --> 02:51:00.747
our ability to survive and fight

02:51:01.247 --> 02:51:04.969
against wildfires. And so this was recommended by the fire officials.

02:51:05.546 --> 02:51:09.440
As something that we could do, and so I'm bringing it to the council

02:51:09.792 --> 02:51:13.701
in that light of explaining this is an option, something we could consider as

02:51:13.958 --> 02:51:18.252
a city to implement. But it's certainly not required by

02:51:18.524 --> 02:51:22.033
the state code or city code or anywhere. This is just an

02:51:22.129 --> 02:51:26.135
option for us. And so after that suggestion, I started

02:51:26.635 --> 02:51:29.836
looking into where else has this been done? Who else?

02:51:29.980 --> 02:51:33.505
Which other jurisdictions are doing this? And most of the places that

02:51:33.521 --> 02:51:37.454
I found. Found were counties outside of Utah. So this

02:51:37.954 --> 02:51:41.067
isn't something that is widely implemented even within the state.

02:51:41.228 --> 02:51:43.572
This would be something a little different for us to do.

02:51:44.664 --> 02:51:48.212
And so we don't have— like, it wouldn't be a copy-paste of

02:51:48.712 --> 02:51:52.692
something done somewhere else. We would want to adapt those standards for

02:51:53.192 --> 02:51:56.305
what would make sense for our city if we chose to go in this direction.

02:51:57.284 --> 02:52:00.753
I thought it was still worth bringing to the council to

02:52:00.801 --> 02:52:04.609
consider this, even though it, it isn't really being done elsewhere.

02:52:05.139 --> 02:52:08.898
Because unlike other cities, a lot of other cities, we're surrounded

02:52:08.930 --> 02:52:12.690
mostly by wildlands, and we don't intend to fully build out.

02:52:12.786 --> 02:52:16.658
We want to protect washes and natural open space.

02:52:17.188 --> 02:52:20.626
And also, we have a lot of large lots, and a lot of those don't

02:52:20.642 --> 02:52:23.935
have fully improved yards. And these are all

02:52:24.435 --> 02:52:28.176
great things for our community, but it does also increase the risk of wildfire

02:52:28.306 --> 02:52:31.711
spreading through our city. So as I

02:52:31.873 --> 02:52:35.959
looked at kind of the pros and cons of this, there are some challenges.

02:52:36.137 --> 02:52:39.920
There's difficulty in defining exactly where we want this to go,

02:52:40.097 --> 02:52:43.559
and we can— the— I call it wooey boundary lots, and we can

02:52:44.059 --> 02:52:47.655
dive into that here in a minute. Now, it could increase the housing

02:52:47.753 --> 02:52:51.334
costs because if we were to put in this greater setback, it would

02:52:51.834 --> 02:52:55.373
most likely need to have a larger lot in order to

02:52:55.873 --> 02:52:59.703
accommodate those. So these boundary lots would then be required for safety

02:52:59.799 --> 02:53:03.382
to be larger. And it could limit and impede

02:53:03.882 --> 02:53:07.452
the placement of accessory structures if we were to choose to include those in the

02:53:07.952 --> 02:53:11.845
standards. But the benefits of this, kind of the reasons we might wanna consider

02:53:11.877 --> 02:53:15.798
this, is it helps protect residents and their properties.

02:53:16.429 --> 02:53:19.594
So the WUI code suggests a 30-foot setback.

02:53:19.776 --> 02:53:23.774
Because usually a 30 or greater setback on

02:53:23.806 --> 02:53:28.029
the property of well-maintained space cleared of vegetation

02:53:28.848 --> 02:53:32.284
drastically improves the chances of that structure surviving

02:53:32.301 --> 02:53:36.251
when a wildfire comes through. And this would create a

02:53:37.439 --> 02:53:40.313
fire perimeter around the city.

02:53:41.293 --> 02:53:44.681
They talk about defensible space when they're— when it

02:53:45.181 --> 02:53:48.501
comes to fire prevention with wildfires. And so

02:53:48.629 --> 02:53:52.348
I think of it kind of cities used to have walls around them

02:53:52.845 --> 02:53:56.131
because the best way to prevent to protect your city is to prevent

02:53:56.631 --> 02:54:00.347
a threat from ever getting inside. So these properties would kind of be the first

02:54:00.379 --> 02:54:04.611
line of defense for that wildfire. So not only would it protect

02:54:04.643 --> 02:54:08.570
their properties, but if we can stop it there, it's not going to get

02:54:08.634 --> 02:54:10.622
inside the city anywhere else.

02:54:11.937 --> 02:54:15.335
And HB 48. From last year,

02:54:16.104 --> 02:54:20.382
properties in that WUI area are now going to be charged fees by

02:54:20.430 --> 02:54:23.795
the state, and they can reduce those fees if they

02:54:24.295 --> 02:54:27.688
do certain things to make their, their,

02:54:28.185 --> 02:54:31.774
um, properties more resilient against wildfires.

02:54:32.351 --> 02:54:35.700
And one of that is to create a 30-foot defensible space.

02:54:36.373 --> 02:54:39.353
But the state code says it's 30 feet or to the property line.

02:54:39.577 --> 02:54:43.119
As, as you raised, okay, if you don't own all 30 feet,

02:54:43.808 --> 02:54:47.014
you can't really clear it. And this takes into

02:54:47.514 --> 02:54:51.374
account, let's say that on the other side is a wash or a hillside.

02:54:51.919 --> 02:54:55.879
We don't want them, you know, you clear out the vegetation,

02:54:55.911 --> 02:54:59.373
that's going to help with fires, but that's going to cause some other issues if

02:54:59.873 --> 02:55:03.044
we start clearing out all the vegetation off the hillsides

02:55:03.544 --> 02:55:07.469
and out of the washes. So this would make sure that they have 30

02:55:07.533 --> 02:55:11.240
feet of defensible space on their property that

02:55:11.304 --> 02:55:14.515
it's— they don't have to worry about another

02:55:15.015 --> 02:55:18.208
property. And hopefully it would help. I know

02:55:18.224 --> 02:55:21.580
we've talked about people haven't been able to qualify for

02:55:22.080 --> 02:55:24.358
fire insurance. And this won't guarantee it.

02:55:25.209 --> 02:55:29.721
But if we're a unique city saying, hey, we're doing something to protect

02:55:30.221 --> 02:55:33.864
against wildfires, hopefully we could help our residents

02:55:34.523 --> 02:55:37.467
be able to qualify for it, for insurance.

02:55:38.243 --> 02:55:41.543
So I wanted to pause here before we take a

02:55:41.559 --> 02:55:44.896
look at any of the code and just do a

02:55:44.961 --> 02:55:48.419
pulse check and see, is this something that the council is

02:55:48.533 --> 02:55:49.718
interested in considering?

02:55:51.848 --> 02:55:55.297
Uh, so my, my home insurance was canceled because of the

02:55:55.394 --> 02:55:59.197
way that the fire department ranks where

02:55:59.697 --> 02:56:02.987
I live. That all gets aggregated into data that goes back

02:56:03.052 --> 02:56:05.251
to the insurance company. Mm-hmm. And they just sent me a letter and said,

02:56:05.765 --> 02:56:09.312
you need a new insurance carrier by next month. And so I had to

02:56:09.537 --> 02:56:12.956
shop. I've got some weird company I've never heard of. They're insuring my house now.

02:56:13.711 --> 02:56:17.596
And I'm just curious if we add maybe a more restrictive

02:56:18.096 --> 02:56:22.187
or more uniform code like this, and I don't see our fire

02:56:22.380 --> 02:56:25.959
people here, could that improve that score

02:56:26.329 --> 02:56:29.766
across our city? I think Brett is online. That's what I would want to

02:56:30.266 --> 02:56:34.536
know. Is he online? He said he was going to try to be there.

02:56:34.761 --> 02:56:38.535
Yeah. Yeah, I'm online. Can you restate

02:56:39.035 --> 02:56:42.342
that question? It kind of got cut off. It wasn't a great question.

02:56:42.471 --> 02:56:46.197
Let me try to rephrase it. If we adopt more

02:56:46.470 --> 02:56:50.148
restrictive policies around our WUI,

02:56:50.807 --> 02:56:54.120
will it help the fire scoring to better

02:56:54.620 --> 02:56:58.343
inform the insurance companies so we have less people losing their home

02:56:58.391 --> 02:57:00.213
insurance policies is my question.

02:57:04.540 --> 02:57:07.966
I— that's a hard question. And I don't have— I

02:57:08.031 --> 02:57:10.520
don't think we have a good answer and I don't think we will have a

02:57:11.020 --> 02:57:15.629
good answer due to the fact that the insurance companies,

02:57:15.661 --> 02:57:19.712
even when municipalities in certain jurisdictions have

02:57:20.212 --> 02:57:22.866
established higher thresholds, It really hasn't.

02:57:23.416 --> 02:57:26.909
Insurance companies have still come in and canceled insurances due

02:57:27.409 --> 02:57:31.357
to the risk and the payouts that have been associated with those risks.

02:57:31.454 --> 02:57:35.046
So I think it would be a hard— I think it

02:57:35.546 --> 02:57:39.286
would be difficult for us to anticipate that at best.

02:57:40.200 --> 02:57:44.247
I would—

02:57:44.425 --> 02:57:48.316
And Dan DeVogue, who's the fire marshal over Our

02:57:48.543 --> 02:57:51.996
area. And historically,

02:57:52.028 --> 02:57:57.862
when you look at fires in general and the history of

02:57:58.362 --> 02:58:02.123
major disasters and fires that have ripped through neighborhoods

02:58:02.156 --> 02:58:05.415
and jurisdictions, you will

02:58:05.915 --> 02:58:09.437
always notice that there are certain houses that are spared. And there's

02:58:09.470 --> 02:58:12.437
been a lot of study that goes into why those have been spared.

02:58:13.003 --> 02:58:16.850
And it almost always comes back to defensible space or God's grace.

02:58:16.930 --> 02:58:19.425
Right. Those are kind of the two things that get pointed to.

02:58:20.950 --> 02:58:24.525
So the biggest thing is defensible space. The 30, 30 to 50

02:58:25.025 --> 02:58:28.358
feet is, is what has been preached for years. And it is

02:58:28.584 --> 02:58:31.810
something that we feel strongly about that will

02:58:31.859 --> 02:58:34.411
save homes ultimately,

02:58:35.155 --> 02:58:38.305
especially if we have a wind-driven, wind-aligned event.

02:58:39.373 --> 02:58:43.110
in high bluey areas. And so that's kind of the

02:58:43.289 --> 02:58:46.753
background behind it. So sorry to go on a diatribe

02:58:47.253 --> 02:58:49.927
outside of the question that was asked. The answer to that question, I don't think

02:58:50.427 --> 02:58:52.696
that we have a good answer. I don't think we will have a good answer.

02:58:53.648 --> 02:58:57.407
And, you know, if we have high insurance payouts, it very

02:58:57.504 --> 02:59:00.988
well may not matter what we do with

02:59:01.166 --> 02:59:04.272
the municipality because insurance companies can kind of— I think that's a great point.

02:59:05.299 --> 02:59:08.748
Now, I know legislatively they're taking a look at different things

02:59:08.812 --> 02:59:12.503
to be able to manage it, but that's kind of what I

02:59:12.568 --> 02:59:15.889
would anticipate. And Marcus may be able to weigh in a little

02:59:16.389 --> 02:59:18.304
bit more in depth on that one.

02:59:21.190 --> 02:59:24.919
I mean, he's not wrong. Insurance companies are going to contract and do business

02:59:25.419 --> 02:59:29.311
with whom they want to. And there's no law in the

02:59:29.811 --> 02:59:33.344
state of Utah that will requires them to insure a specific home.

02:59:33.505 --> 02:59:37.097
Yeah, my understanding was that there are scores.

02:59:37.516 --> 02:59:40.003
Right. I mean, it wouldn't hurt.

02:59:40.683 --> 02:59:43.874
It's a question of whether it would help. It's not going to hurt.

02:59:44.392 --> 02:59:47.858
The ISO rating is a component of the fire response times,

02:59:48.370 --> 02:59:52.537
a component of the water system and the water availability into a given neighborhood.

02:59:53.213 --> 02:59:56.615
And all of these type of things is a comprehensive look. We actually have a

02:59:57.115 --> 03:00:00.645
pretty high ISO rating right now, which is pretty good.

03:00:01.031 --> 03:00:04.563
But different areas of town, and depending on how close you are to a hydrant

03:00:05.063 --> 03:00:08.336
and all that kind of thing, can affect insurance

03:00:08.577 --> 03:00:12.367
companies. Nothing can compel them to actually insure,

03:00:12.575 --> 03:00:15.963
but I think you're seeing nationwide this happening all over

03:00:16.463 --> 03:00:19.191
the place. For sure. When I was in Iowa,

03:00:20.299 --> 03:00:24.698
companies were just pulling out of the state just for no other reason than tornadoes

03:00:25.037 --> 03:00:28.441
and ripping shingles off roofs and whatnot.

03:00:28.521 --> 03:00:31.772
So insurance companies are becoming more and more risk-averse.

03:00:33.690 --> 03:00:37.337
So I guess the question then is the real benefit that

03:00:37.837 --> 03:00:40.597
we can hope to help them with is what they're being fined,

03:00:41.442 --> 03:00:44.312
and then obviously the 30-foot barriers,

03:00:45.005 --> 03:00:48.735
trying to prevent that from taking over the city. My concern

03:00:49.235 --> 03:00:52.347
is going to obviously be their right to use their property.

03:00:53.520 --> 03:00:57.116
I mean, some people, that's their entire backyard.

03:00:58.176 --> 03:01:01.195
So I mean, I think that people are going to have some of those

03:01:01.243 --> 03:01:04.245
concerns. I stated before, I feel like this,

03:01:05.723 --> 03:01:09.271
this bill didn't go far enough. It's penalizing those that

03:01:09.351 --> 03:01:13.012
bought a house that they had nothing to do with how big the backyard was,

03:01:13.831 --> 03:01:17.428
and the adjacent owners don't have any responsibility to do

03:01:17.476 --> 03:01:20.061
a fire line that goes up against those properties.

03:01:20.816 --> 03:01:24.477
I would really like the state to have a more broad approach

03:01:24.525 --> 03:01:27.993
and say, we don't know when those are going to be developed,

03:01:28.041 --> 03:01:31.317
and so there should be 30 feet of fire line that's cut,

03:01:31.493 --> 03:01:35.026
a road at the very minimum that protects the houses from the

03:01:35.526 --> 03:01:38.558
structure from going through your property, which it's absolutely going to go through because that's

03:01:39.058 --> 03:01:42.553
where the weeds are and the sagebrush and so forth. But yeah,

03:01:42.569 --> 03:01:45.392
I mean, I, in principle, I think it's a good idea.

03:01:46.499 --> 03:01:50.013
Uh, I'm not enthusiastic about taking people's sheds,

03:01:50.510 --> 03:01:53.382
barns, and that kind of thing down and saying you can't have those.

03:01:54.201 --> 03:01:57.746
Um, I'd love to see other options that they can do

03:01:58.246 --> 03:02:01.099
to make them fire resistant as part of that plan if possible,

03:02:01.115 --> 03:02:04.325
but that'd be my only 2 cents

03:02:04.825 --> 03:02:07.198
on that. Um, just, just, uh,

03:02:07.600 --> 03:02:11.372
I'll respond to him and then we'll go to, um, Council Member

03:02:11.872 --> 03:02:14.824
Clark. So we do have it in there. Well, first,

03:02:14.920 --> 03:02:18.420
it would only apply to the O's that require a building permit.

03:02:19.110 --> 03:02:22.305
So that means they're over 200 square feet or they have

03:02:22.805 --> 03:02:26.350
electrical or plumbing to them. But we also do have the option

03:02:27.265 --> 03:02:30.894
that if they were to— that they could get an approval if they were

03:02:31.394 --> 03:02:35.100
to have them be— have fire safety taken into

03:02:35.133 --> 03:02:38.713
account. So like if they did a metal shed or other fire-resistant material,

03:02:39.628 --> 03:02:43.113
then they would— we would allow exceptions for that reason.

03:02:43.161 --> 03:02:46.276
Because we don't— we wanted this to be about fire protection,

03:02:46.854 --> 03:02:50.467
not doing that. And the Planning Commission did suggest

03:02:50.547 --> 03:02:53.935
adding accessory dwellings in there. Of course, if the council

03:02:54.435 --> 03:02:57.433
doesn't want accessory dwellings, we could Accessory building, sorry.

03:02:58.016 --> 03:03:01.204
We could take that out. I think for me,

03:03:01.658 --> 03:03:08.046
there's been so many times where a project's come before us— Oh. That was

03:03:09.855 --> 03:03:11.195
Amber? Was that Brett? Go ahead.

03:03:15.488 --> 03:03:19.149
Maybe not. Yeah, sorry. It's hard to talk.

03:03:19.882 --> 03:03:22.120
No, can you hear me? Yeah, go ahead.

03:03:25.720 --> 03:03:28.618
Yeah, sorry, Council Member Clark, I didn't mean to cut you off. Um, one of

03:03:28.715 --> 03:03:32.596
the other things to keep in mind too is this is, this is directly related

03:03:32.677 --> 03:03:36.506
to the, the WUI high-risk areas and new builds within those

03:03:36.619 --> 03:03:39.559
areas. Uh, and so it's not specifically,

03:03:40.378 --> 03:03:44.347
um, going to take hold, you know, in throughout

03:03:44.395 --> 03:03:47.632
the city or in all properties. It's primarily going

03:03:48.132 --> 03:03:50.991
to be in those high- high-risk areas that have already been identified by the state

03:03:51.491 --> 03:03:54.828
that are still going to get taxed and, and have some fee assessed.

03:03:55.390 --> 03:03:58.906
This will help, uh, to protect those areas

03:03:59.083 --> 03:04:02.695
as they do builds. So, uh, Councilmember Clark, I apologize to cut

03:04:03.195 --> 03:04:06.628
you off. No worries. It's hard when we have delays and different

03:04:07.128 --> 03:04:09.952
things. So what I started to say was there's been so many projects that have

03:04:10.452 --> 03:04:13.708
come before me, and I think all of us where we, we can look at

03:04:14.208 --> 03:04:18.255
it and we can say that's not going to work because

03:04:18.625 --> 03:04:21.870
someone's trying to get the most lots out of it humanly possible,

03:04:22.690 --> 03:04:26.129
and we know that the wash is going to impede something,

03:04:26.466 --> 03:04:30.387
the slope is going to impede something, and now we have

03:04:31.046 --> 03:04:34.774
the WUI adjacent setback that would— and the fire risk,

03:04:34.918 --> 03:04:38.453
I guess, is the best way to say that. And we can only say no

03:04:39.000 --> 03:04:42.913
or force something with very specific things: health,

03:04:43.010 --> 03:04:46.359
safety, and welfare. And this is very specifically for

03:04:46.391 --> 03:04:50.044
safety. This is a tool that allows us as

03:04:51.342 --> 03:04:54.723
a land use decision body that to look at a

03:04:55.223 --> 03:04:58.168
project and say, we have this setback now,

03:04:58.729 --> 03:05:02.703
and for safety reasons, we're going to require that these lots are bigger

03:05:02.896 --> 03:05:06.773
or there's bigger setbacks. And I get that that will increase

03:05:07.078 --> 03:05:11.125
some lots' prices, but the reality of it is, is not every

03:05:11.221 --> 03:05:15.107
lot in every city is made for every financial

03:05:15.607 --> 03:05:19.859
situation. And the people who could have a lot like this were—

03:05:20.052 --> 03:05:23.328
are going to be in a better position to have that setback and to leave

03:05:23.360 --> 03:05:27.374
that space and have that defensible space. For that

03:05:27.874 --> 03:05:31.292
reason, I'm inclined to approve this because it's another really

03:05:31.792 --> 03:05:35.461
good tool for us as decision makers to make sure that our neighborhoods,

03:05:35.558 --> 03:05:38.974
especially the ones adjacent to WUI, are safer.

03:05:40.231 --> 03:05:42.147
Just a follow-up question. Um,

03:05:43.709 --> 03:05:47.170
maybe this is obvious to everybody else but not to me. So the

03:05:47.670 --> 03:05:50.793
WUI can exist within our city boundaries. It's not just the outward boundaries that we're

03:05:51.293 --> 03:05:54.599
talking about. That being the case, they can fall out

03:05:55.099 --> 03:05:58.925
of that as well as the next subdivision is built. So the

03:05:59.425 --> 03:06:02.136
question that I'm thinking of in my mind is, what are we doing to notify

03:06:02.636 --> 03:06:04.721
the people who are buying the house that can't do anything with their backyard?

03:06:05.733 --> 03:06:08.575
Because I can promise you the developer's not going to tell them that. Yeah.

03:06:09.265 --> 03:06:12.268
I think if we're going to implement something like that, there needs to be some

03:06:12.894 --> 03:06:16.764
responsibility placed on those who are building the property to let

03:06:17.264 --> 03:06:20.569
that be known as part of the process, that you are in a WUI

03:06:20.907 --> 03:06:23.299
area 30 feet of your backyard.

03:06:24.387 --> 03:06:28.274
is not buildable for any accessory, anything back

03:06:28.774 --> 03:06:33.116
there. Because look, there's a lot of things we don't have to take responsibility

03:06:33.616 --> 03:06:36.127
for— people's views and the sky above and any of those kind of things.

03:06:36.192 --> 03:06:39.171
But if you're going to put a restriction like that on the property, they need

03:06:39.671 --> 03:06:43.534
to be notified in a way that's meaningful. So this would only impact new properties,

03:06:43.582 --> 03:06:46.948
so new builds. So it would be something that we, we would be

03:06:47.448 --> 03:06:50.885
looking at as we're approving Plots and and

03:06:52.218 --> 03:06:55.285
and developments. That's what I'm looking at. I'm looking at the ones that are coming

03:06:55.879 --> 03:06:59.572
into it, and they're saying, "Great, I got this brand new house I built." What

03:07:00.072 --> 03:07:03.538
I didn't know is that I can't put anything in my backyard. I think it's

03:07:04.038 --> 03:07:06.733
important that that if they're in the WUI that we need to let them know

03:07:07.233 --> 03:07:10.234
that they exist in that zone. That needs to be something that is required of

03:07:10.266 --> 03:07:14.119
the builder to let them know. Also, to let them know that as the

03:07:14.619 --> 03:07:18.032
next development is done, they will fall out of that. But for whatever

03:07:18.532 --> 03:07:21.382
that period of time is, like, we need to help them to be aware of

03:07:21.414 --> 03:07:24.652
what they're saying yes to. So if we're putting the restriction there,

03:07:25.646 --> 03:07:28.980
I will— let's dive into the code because I,

03:07:30.214 --> 03:07:33.580
I realize this might need some more work, and I'm fine if we table it

03:07:34.080 --> 03:07:37.219
and come back. I wanted to start— have a starting

03:07:37.283 --> 03:07:40.633
point because I figure it's easier to start with something than to

03:07:41.133 --> 03:07:44.537
start with nothing. And my hope is to try to impact as few

03:07:44.925 --> 03:07:48.176
properties as possible and not have

03:07:48.224 --> 03:07:51.427
the properties who are falling out of it for it to apply to them.

03:07:51.976 --> 03:07:55.485
So that did make some of the trying to define things a little

03:07:55.582 --> 03:07:58.831
tricky. But when I talked about that wall,

03:07:59.400 --> 03:08:02.647
if you think about it that way, it would only be

03:08:03.147 --> 03:08:06.811
the properties on the wall. So if you have a property and

03:08:06.827 --> 03:08:10.279
right now it's the last property between you and a hillside,

03:08:10.616 --> 03:08:13.811
but there's developable and buildable land behind you,

03:08:14.405 --> 03:08:18.227
that means it's going to be large enough and level enough to put in

03:08:18.805 --> 03:08:22.193
housing or a business. Then that's a defensible

03:08:22.693 --> 03:08:26.688
space for the city, so it wouldn't apply to you. This only

03:08:27.188 --> 03:08:30.751
applies to people if you are up against a wash or a hillside,

03:08:31.184 --> 03:08:34.459
the very perimeter of the city Where we don't

03:08:34.523 --> 03:08:37.988
have— there's behind you, that land is never slated to be

03:08:38.488 --> 03:08:42.159
developed. This— the people it might— that it could impact is

03:08:42.659 --> 03:08:46.683
if we annexed in later. So those people may end up with a bigger setback

03:08:47.453 --> 03:08:51.127
and then development goes in behind them, but it's not meant,

03:08:51.223 --> 03:08:55.025
oh, you're Phase 1, so you have to have a 30-foot setback,

03:08:55.089 --> 03:08:58.121
and then Phase 2 goes in and the edge of that has to have a

03:08:58.621 --> 03:09:02.167
30-foot setback. But it could be a Phase 2 It could be,

03:09:02.311 --> 03:09:05.508
but again, if that land's large enough and level

03:09:05.604 --> 03:09:08.881
enough, we would be able to defend the city from

03:09:08.961 --> 03:09:12.414
there. So that was my thought process. We can change that,

03:09:13.137 --> 03:09:16.173
but trying to— again, we— this is for safety.

03:09:16.687 --> 03:09:20.413
If we can accomplish safety without impeding their

03:09:21.088 --> 03:09:24.461
land rights, then we want to do that. I think

03:09:24.961 --> 03:09:28.331
one of the questions that I have about this, Beth, is— and I'm thinking specifically

03:09:28.831 --> 03:09:31.960
of Circle 5. Circle 5 is in the WUI. I think it's identified as

03:09:32.460 --> 03:09:36.423
a WUI-5 area, and we know that there are entitlements in that area. And I'm

03:09:36.923 --> 03:09:40.036
just thinking, how's the developer who already has some existing entitlements in there,

03:09:40.052 --> 03:09:43.890
how's he going to make this function in an

03:09:44.390 --> 03:09:48.370
existing area? So I mean, I kind of— I'm hearing

03:09:48.386 --> 03:09:52.116
all of this, and what I'm hearing is a desire

03:09:52.181 --> 03:09:56.897
to think about how could we create

03:09:56.962 --> 03:10:01.507
safer areas in the city, and I think that's an admirable desire.

03:10:02.349 --> 03:10:05.866
But I also think we need to look at what's the real-world impact to

03:10:06.093 --> 03:10:09.293
existing entitlements and

03:10:09.486 --> 03:10:12.938
to those future entitlements. And if it's just gonna pertain to future and

